[[:db/cas 17592186553525 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-13x-6y=24 \\\\ \\,8x-5y=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAccording to Cramer's rule, the unknown $x$ can be written as \n$x=\\frac{{D}_{x}}{|\\begin{aligned}-13 & -6 \\\\ 8 & -5\\end{aligned}|}$, where ${D}_{x}=$" "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-13x-6y=24 \\\\ \\,8x-5y=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAccording to Cramer's rule, the unknown $x$ can be written as \n$$\n\\displaystyle x=\\frac{{D}_{x}}{|\\begin{aligned}-13 & -6 \\\\ 8 & -5\\end{aligned}|},\n$$ where ${D}_{x}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186553528 :question/problem "Consider the vector-valued function $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{3}$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhat is $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x},$ the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]?$" "Consider the vector-valued function $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{3}$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhat is $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x},$ the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186553528 :question/worked-solution "The total derivative of a vector-valued function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ with respect to a vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{m\\times n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, writing out the function $f(x)$ where $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{2}$, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ 3{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, the total derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}(5{x}_{1}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}(5{x}_{1}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}(3{x}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}(3{x}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The total derivative of a vector-valued function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ with respect to a vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{m\\times n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, writing out the function $f(x)$ where $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{2},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ 3{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, the total derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}(5{x}_{1}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}(5{x}_{1}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}(3{x}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}(3{x}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186553534 :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s$ along any closed curve $C⊂D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}⊂D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nNote that $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i+2xy\\,j$ is a vector field with the simply connected domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P(x,y)={x}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=2xy.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2x+2x=4x\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statements II and III are false. Indeed, this follows from statement I.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s\n$$ along any closed curve $C⊂D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}⊂D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nNote that $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i+2xy\\,j$ is a vector field with the simply connected domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P(x,y)={x}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=2xy.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2x+2x=4x\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statements II and III are false. Indeed, this follows from statement I.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186553543 :question/worked-solution "To solve the initial value problem, we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of $Y(s).$\n\nTo find the inverse transform, we first ignore the exponential term ${e}^{-\\pi s/2}$ (which corresponds to a time shift) and focus on the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{2}{({s}^{2}+4)(s+1)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains a linear factor $(s+1)$ and an irreducible quadratic factor $({s}^{2}+4),$ the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{({s}^{2}+4)(s+1)}=\\frac{A}{s+1}+\\frac{Bs+C}{{s}^{2}+4}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n2=A({s}^{2}+4)+(Bs+C)(s+1).\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A,B,$ and $C.$\n\n- Setting $s=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n2=A((-1)^{2}+4)+0\\,⟹\\,5A=2\\,⟹\\,A=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of ${s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n0=A+B\\,⟹\\,B=-A=-\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the constant terms, we have\n\n$$\n2=4A+C\\,⟹\\,C=2-\\frac{8}{5}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{2}{5}(\\frac{1}{s+1}+\\frac{-s+1}{{s}^{2}+4})=\\frac{2}{5}(\\frac{1}{s+1}-\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}+\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+4}).\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t)={L}^{-1}\\{F(s)\\}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\frac{2}{5}({L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s+1}\\}-{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}\\}+{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+4}\\}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}({e}^{-t}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-\\pi s/2}$ term in $Y(s).$ By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)\\}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}\\{{e}^{-as}F(s)\\}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})f\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot \\frac{2}{5}[{e}^{-(t-\\pi /2)}-cos\\,(2\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))+\\frac{1}{2}sin\\,(2\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the trigonometric identities $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-\\pi /2))=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-\\pi /2))=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),$ we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot \\frac{2}{5}[{e}^{-(t-\\pi /2)}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\,u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})(2{e}^{-(t-\\pi /2)}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the form given in the problem statement $y(t)=\\frac{1}{5}u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t)=2{e}^{-(t-\\pi /2)}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$" "To solve the initial value problem, we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of $Y(s).$\n\nTo find the inverse transform, we first ignore the exponential term ${e}^{-\\pi s/2}$ (which corresponds to a time shift) and focus on the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{2}{({s}^{2}+4)(s+1)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains a linear factor $(s+1)$ and an irreducible quadratic factor $({s}^{2}+4),$ the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{({s}^{2}+4)(s+1)}=\\frac{A}{s+1}+\\frac{Bs+C}{{s}^{2}+4}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n2=A({s}^{2}+4)+(Bs+C)(s+1).\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A,B,$ and $C.$\n\n- Setting $s=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n2=A((-1)^{2}+4)+0\\,⟹\\,5A=2\\,⟹\\,A=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of ${s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n0=A+B\\,⟹\\,B=-A=-\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the constant terms, we have\n\n$$\n2=4A+C\\,⟹\\,C=2-\\frac{8}{5}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{2}{5}(\\frac{1}{s+1}+\\frac{-s+1}{{s}^{2}+4})=\\frac{2}{5}(\\frac{1}{s+1}-\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}+\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+4}).\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t)={L}^{-1}\\{F(s)\\}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\frac{2}{5}({L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s+1}\\}-{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}\\}+{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+4}\\}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}({e}^{-t}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-\\pi s/2}$ term in $Y(s).$ By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)\\}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}\\{{e}^{-as}F(s)\\}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})f\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot \\frac{2}{5}[{e}^{-(t-\\pi /2)}-cos\\,(2\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))+\\frac{1}{2}sin\\,(2\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the trigonometric identities $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-\\pi /2))=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-\\pi /2))=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),$ we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot \\frac{2}{5}[{e}^{-(t-\\pi /2)}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\,u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})(2{e}^{-(t-\\pi /2)}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the form given in the problem statement $$\n\\displaystyle y(t)=\\frac{1}{5}u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot g(t),\n$$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t)=2{e}^{-(t-\\pi /2)}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186553561 :question/problem "Consider the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by $f(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{3}.$ What is the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]?$" "Consider the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by $f(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{3}.$ What is the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186553574 :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s$ along any closed curve $C\\in D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}\\in D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-\\sqrt{{y}^{3}})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}((x-1)^{2})=0+0=0,\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\nSince $F$ is source-free, we know the flux of $F$ is independent of the path. In particular,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{2}$ is the straight line from $A(1,3)$ to $B(5,3)$ and ${n}_{2}$ is its unit normal, shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/3d/3d64ffb0e4cc313a2857d1c20922c1202619d0bdeb3d97f2808b4995c99f7f9f.png)\n\nNotice that both ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotations of their respective unit tangent vectors.\n\nTo calculate the flux of $F$ across ${C}_{2},$ we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply the formula,\n\n- the curve should be parametrized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector ${n}_{2}$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit normal tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nParametrizing ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =i+3\\,j+t\\cdot [(5\\,i+3\\,j)-(i+3\\,j)] \\\\  & =(4t+1)\\,i+3\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in [0,1].$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =-\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}=-\\sqrt{(3)^{3}}=-\\sqrt{27}, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =(x-1)^{2}=((4t+1)-1)^{2}=16{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t+1)=4, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the flux integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\sqrt{27})\\cdot (0)-(16{t}^{2})\\cdot (4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-64{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{64{t}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{64}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s=-\\frac{64}{3}.\n$$" "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s\n$$ along any closed curve $C\\in D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}\\in D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-\\sqrt{{y}^{3}})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}((x-1)^{2})=0+0=0,\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\nSince $F$ is source-free, we know the flux of $F$ is independent of the path. In particular,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{2}$ is the straight line from $A(1,3)$ to $B(5,3)$ and ${n}_{2}$ is its unit normal, shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/3d/3d64ffb0e4cc313a2857d1c20922c1202619d0bdeb3d97f2808b4995c99f7f9f.png)\n\nNotice that both ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotations of their respective unit tangent vectors.\n\nTo calculate the flux of $F$ across ${C}_{2},$ we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply the formula,\n\n- the curve should be parametrized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector ${n}_{2}$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit normal tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nParametrizing ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =i+3\\,j+t\\cdot [(5\\,i+3\\,j)-(i+3\\,j)] \\\\  & =(4t+1)\\,i+3\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in [0,1].$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =-\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}=-\\sqrt{(3)^{3}}=-\\sqrt{27}, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =(x-1)^{2}=((4t+1)-1)^{2}=16{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t+1)=4, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the flux integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\sqrt{27})\\cdot (0)-(16{t}^{2})\\cdot (4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-64{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{64{t}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{64}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s=-\\frac{64}{3}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186553584 :question/problem "Consider the vector-valued function $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{4}$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 5\\end{aligned}]x.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhat is $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x},$ the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ at ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]?$" "Consider the vector-valued function $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{4}$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 5\\end{aligned}]x.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhat is $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x},$ the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ at $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186553584 :question/worked-solution "The total derivative of a vector-valued function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ with respect to a vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{m\\times n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, writing out the function $f(x)$ where $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{2}$, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2} \\\\ 6{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, the total derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{4}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{4}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}(3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}(3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}(4{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}(4{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}(6{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}(6{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the total derivative is a constant matrix, evaluating it at ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$ gives the same matrix." "The total derivative of a vector-valued function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ with respect to a vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{m\\times n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, writing out the function $f(x)$ where $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{2},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2} \\\\ 6{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, the total derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{4}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{4}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}(3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}(3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}(4{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}(4{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}(6{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}(6{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the total derivative is a constant matrix, evaluating it at $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$ gives the same matrix."] [:db/cas 17592186553588 :question/problem "![](images/a1/a1f53be1baf6a5db5807565f0511813cb41a7b48ec42e37a96049403aaa2235d.png)\n\nThe area under the curve $y=\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}}$ between $x=0$, $x=5$ and the $x$-axis is approximated using a 