nt from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $(0,3),$ as shown above." "![](images/4d/4d83f771aa650879909c33d7033e80ca044b7e6f8369e19667e6173aa8a40737.png)\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}7\\left(x+\\sqrt{3y}\\right)\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$ where $C$ is the path traversed along the line segment from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $(0,3),$ as shown above."] [:db/cas 17592186554550 :question/problem "Let $\\widehat{\\,y}={\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=12.6-3.9x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $({x}_{1},{y}_{1}),\\,…,\\,({x}_{18},{y}_{18})$ of $n=18$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=3.5,$ the mean square error of the model is $2.1,$ and ${S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}=85,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.921)=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $16$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for ${\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\n*" "Let $\\widehat{\\,y}={\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=12.6-3.9x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $({x}_{1},{y}_{1}),\\,…,\\,({x}_{18},{y}_{18})$ of $n=18$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=3.5,$ the mean square error of the model is $2.1,$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}=85,\n$$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.921)=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $16$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for ${\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\n*"] [:db/cas 17592186554552 :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-3I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-3I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186554563 :question/problem "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -10 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{23}=$" "Given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -10 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{23}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186554568 :question/worked-solution "![](images/23/236144ff93bd22f5c4460c31e20216c45d6f8ac85dc945321dadecc093f379e0.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the kite's height, and $z$ is the distance between the boy and the kite. The quantity $z=20$ meters does not change with time because the string's length doesn't change.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.$\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $y$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $y$ and $z$ are the opposite and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{z} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{20} \\\\ y & =20\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(20\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =(20\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConverting the given information from degrees to radians, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=1$ and $\\theta ={45}^{∘}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{{180}^{∘}}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Substituting this into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =(20\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right))\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{20} & =\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the angle of elevation is increasing at a rate of $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{20}$ radians per second." "![](images/23/236144ff93bd22f5c4460c31e20216c45d6f8ac85dc945321dadecc093f379e0.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the kite's height, and $z$ is the distance between the boy and the kite. The quantity $z=20$ meters does not change with time because the string's length doesn't change.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.$\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $y$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $y$ and $z$ are the opposite and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{z} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{20} \\\\ y & =20\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(20\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =(20\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConverting the given information from degrees to radians, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=1$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\theta ={45}^{∘}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{{180}^{∘}}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$ Substituting this into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =(20\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right))\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{20} & =\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the angle of elevation is increasing at a rate of $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{20}$ radians per second."] [:db/cas 17592186554569 :question/problem "Let $\\widehat{\\,y}={\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=3.4+1.8x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $({x}_{1},{y}_{1}),\\,…,\\,({x}_{12},{y}_{12})$ of $n=12$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, the mean square error of the model is $11.5,$ and ${S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}=143,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the slope of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>1.812)=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $10$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for ${\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}.\n$$\n\n*" "Let $\\widehat{\\,y}={\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=3.4+1.8x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $({x}_{1},{y}_{1}),\\,…,\\,({x}_{12},{y}_{12})$ of $n=12$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, the mean square error of the model is $11.5,$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}=143,\n$$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the slope of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>1.812)=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $10$ degree