\\displaystyle {S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}=484,\n$$ find a $90%$  confidence interval for the slope of the regression line. \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>1.746)=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $16$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for ${\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}.\n$$\n\n*"] [:db/cas 17592186554623 :question/worked-solution "Let's define  $f(x,y,z)={e}^{xy}-{x}^{2}-2{z}^{2}.$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=e-3.$  \n\nNow, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $(1,1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{xy}-{x}^{2}-2{z}^{2})i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{xy}-{x}^{2}-2{z}^{2}))j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{xy}-{x}^{2}-2{z}^{2})k \\\\  & =(y{e}^{xy}-2x)i+(x{e}^{xy})j+(-4z)k \\\\ ∇f(1,1,-1) & =((1){e}^{(1)(1)}-2(1))\\text{ }i+(1){e}^{(1)(1)}\\text{ }j+(-4)(-1)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =(e-2)\\text{ }i+e\\text{ }j+4\\text{ }k \\\\  & =⟨e-2,\\,e,\\,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our normal vector is $n=⟨a,b,c⟩=⟨e-2,\\,e,\\,4⟩.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a-b}{c}=\\frac{(e-2)-e}{4}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$" "Let's define  $f(x,y,z)={e}^{xy}-{x}^{2}-2{z}^{2}.$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=e-3.$  \n\nNow, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $(1,1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{xy}-{x}^{2}-2{z}^{2})i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{xy}-{x}^{2}-2{z}^{2}))j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{xy}-{x}^{2}-2{z}^{2})k \\\\  & =(y{e}^{xy}-2x)i+(x{e}^{xy})j+(-4z)k \\\\ ∇f(1,1,-1) & =((1){e}^{(1)(1)}-2(1))\\text{ }i+(1){e}^{(1)(1)}\\text{ }j+(-4)(-1)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =(e-2)\\text{ }i+e\\text{ }j+4\\text{ }k \\\\  & =⟨e-2,\\,e,\\,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our normal vector is $n=⟨a,b,c⟩=⟨e-2,\\,e,\\,4⟩.$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{a-b}{c}=\\frac{(e-2)-e}{4}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186554626 :question/problem "$$\n{x}_{1}=-6,\\,{x}_{2}=-1,\\,{x}_{3}=0,\\,{x}_{4}=4,\\,{x}_{5}=7\n$$\n\nGiven the values ${x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3},\\,{x}_{4},\\,{x}_{5}$ above, we have\n\n${\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}\\ge 0]\\cdot (2{x}_{i})+1[{x}_{i}<0]\\cdot 1=$ {{field-1}}." "$$\n{x}_{1}=-6,\\,{x}_{2}=-1,\\,{x}_{3}=0,\\,{x}_{4}=4,\\,{x}_{5}=7\n$$\n\nGiven the values ${x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3},\\,{x}_{4},\\,{x}_{5}$ above, we have\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}\\ge 0]\\cdot (2{x}_{i})+1[{x}_{i}<0]\\cdot 1=\n$$ {{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186554636 :question/problem "$$\n{x}_{1}=9,\\,{x}_{2}=-8,\\,{x}_{3}=-1,\\,{x}_{4}=4,\\,{x}_{5}=2\n$$\n\nGiven the values ${x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3},\\,{x}_{4},\\,{x}_{5}$ above, we have\n\n${\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}>0]\\cdot {x}_{i}=$ {{field-1}}." "$$\n{x}_{1}=9,\\,{x}_{2}=-8,\\,{x}_{3}=-1,\\,{x}_{4}=4,\\,{x}_{5}=2\n$$\n\nGiven the values ${x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3},\\,{x}_{4},\\,{x}_{5}$ above, we have\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}>0]\\cdot {x}_{i}=\n$$ {{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186554638 :question/problem "Let $\\widehat{\\,y}={\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=2.8-3.3x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $({x}_{1},{y}_{1}),\\,…,\\,({x}_{10},{y}_{10})$ of $n=10$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=2.7,$ the mean square error of the model is $3.6,$ and ${S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}=82.5,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>3.355)=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $8$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for ${\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdo