ntal matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c \\\\  & ={e}^{At}\\,c \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.$" "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 9 \\\\ 9 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$ So, the matrix of the system is $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 9 \\\\ 9 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c \\\\  & ={e}^{At}\\,c \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.$"] [:db/cas 17592186555762 :question/problem "Consider $E=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A.$ Find the row operation that, when applied to $A,$ is equivalent to multiplying $A$ by the given elementary matrix $E$ from the left." "Consider $$\n\\displaystyle E=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$ and a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A.$ Find the row operation that, when applied to $A,$ is equivalent to multiplying $A$ by the given elementary matrix $E$ from the left."] [:db/cas 17592186555769 :question/problem "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -8 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$" "Given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -8 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186555771 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $3\\times 3$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the first column must be orthogonal to the second and third columns, and\n\n- the norm of the first column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{7} \\\\ \\frac{6}{7} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{7}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties." "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $3\\times 3$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the first column must be orthogonal to the second and third columns, and\n\n- the norm of the first column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $$\n\\displaystyle [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{7} \\\\ \\frac{6}{7} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{7}\\end{aligned}]\n$$ has both properties."] [:db/cas 17592186555773 :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4,\\,y-4\\le z\\le 3\\},\n$$\n\nthen ${∭}_{R}z\\,\\text{d}V=$" "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4,\\,y-4\\le z\\le 3\\},\n$$\n\nthen $$\n\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}z\\,\\text{d}V=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186555774 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=5y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*" "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=5y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}],\n$$ where $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$*"] [:db/cas 17592186555774 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,x(0),\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system, and $x(0)$ is the initial condition vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5t) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$ So, the matrix of the system is $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,x(0),\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system, and $x(0)$ is the initial condition vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5t) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186555780 :question/problem "Consider $E=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A.$ Find the row operation that, when applied to $A,$ is equivalent to multiplying $A$ by the given elementary matrix $E$ from the left." "Consider $$\n\\displaystyle E=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$ and a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A.$ Find the row operation that, when applied to $A,$ is equivalent to multiplying $A$ by the given elementary matrix $E$ from the left."] [:db/cas 17592186555789 :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\nII. $[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle \\{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle [\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $$\n\\displaystyle \\{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle \\{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$"] [:db/cas 17592186555791 :question/problem "Consider $E=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A.$ Find the row operation that, when applied to $A,$ is equivalent to multiplying $A$ by the given elementary matrix $E$ from the left." "Consider $$\n\\displaystyle E=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$ and a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A.$ Find the row operation that, when applied to $A,$ is equivalent to multiplying $A$ by the given elementary matrix $E$ from the left."] [:db/cas 17592186555795 :question/problem "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 9\\end{aligned}]$ \nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{22}=$" "Given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$ \nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{22}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186555799 :question/problem "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=200$ from a population where $60%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $i=1,…,200.$ Find an approximate sampling distribution of the statistic $X={\\sum }_{i=1}^{200}{X}_{i}.$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=200$ from a population where $60%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $i=1,…,200.$ Find an approximate sampling distribution of the statistic $$\n\\displaystyle X={\\sum }_{i=1}^{200}{X}_{i}.\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*"] [:db/cas 17592186555807 :question/problem "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $A?$\n\nI. $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nII. $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]$\n\nIII. $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $A?$\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186555810 :question/problem "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=80$ from a population where $72%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $i=1,…,80.$ Find an approximate sampling distribution of the statistic $X={\\sum }_{i=1}^{80}{X}_{i}.$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=80$ from a population where $72%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $i=1,…,80.$ Find an approximate sampling distribution of the statistic $$\n\\displaystyle X={\\sum }_{i=1}^{80}{X}_{i}.\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*"] [:db/cas 17592186555811 :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}\\le 4,\\,-y-3\\le z\\le 3\\},\n$$\n\nthen ${∭}_{R}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}V=$" "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}\\le 4,\\,-y-3\\le z\\le 3\\},\n$$\n\nthen $$\n\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}V=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186555847 :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\nII. $[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle \\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle [\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $$\n\\displaystyle \\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle \\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$"] [:db/cas 17592186555847 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ So the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]=2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ can't be written as a linear combination of \n\n$$\n\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nsince all these linear combinations result in matrices with the entry ${a}_{11}={a}_{22}:$\n\n$$\na\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+b\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+c\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & a\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ So the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]=2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, the matrix $$\n\\displaystyle [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$ can't be written as a linear combination of \n\n$$\n\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nsince all these linear combinations result in matrices with the entry ${a}_{11}={a}_{22}:$\n\n$$\na\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+b\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+c\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & a\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186555856 :question/problem "Given that\n $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ -12 & -9\\end{aligned}]$ \nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$" "Given that\n $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ -12 & -9\\end{aligned}]\n$$ \nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186555867 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(2) \\\\ y(2)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"130px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(2) \\\\ y(2)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}],\n$$ where $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"130px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [:db/cas 17592186555867 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nSo, we can write our general solution as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system. Substituting $t={t}_{0},$ we have\n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c,\n$$\n\nand since ${e}^{At}$ is invertible for all $t,$ we have\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nPlugging the above back into our expression for $x(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{A(t-2)}\\,x(2) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3(t-2)) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(t-2)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(t-2)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3(t-2))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3(t-2))\\, \\\\ \\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(t-2))\\,\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$ So, the matrix of the system is $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nSo, we can write our general solution as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system. Substituting $t={t}_{0},$ we have\n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c,\n$$\n\nand since ${e}^{At}$ is invertible for all $t,$ we have\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nPlugging the above back into our expression for $x(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{A(t-2)}\\,x(2) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3(t-2)) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(t-2)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(t-2)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3(t-2))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3(t-2))\\, \\\\ \\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(t-2))\\,\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186555870 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=9\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-8$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-9I & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -7\\end{aligned}]-9[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-8I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{17},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-8)I & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -7\\end{aligned}]+8[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}16 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-8)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{17},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=9\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-8$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-9I & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -7\\end{aligned}]-9[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-8I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{17},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-8)I & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -7\\end{aligned}]+8[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}16 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-8)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{17},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186557213 :question/problem "Find a matrix that defines the orthogonal projection of ${R}^{3}$ onto the subspace spanned by $a=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$" "Find a matrix that defines the orthogonal projection of ${R}^{3}$ onto the subspace spanned by $$\n\\displaystyle a=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186557213 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the matrix ${P}_{S}=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}$ maps any vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ onto its orthogonal projection in the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of the matrix $A.$ \n\nIn our case, $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S} & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{([\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={6}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that the matrix ${P}_{S}=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}$ maps any vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ onto its orthogonal projection in the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of the matrix $A.$ \n\nIn our case, $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S} & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{([\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={6}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186557244 :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{2}{\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)}^{x}?$" "Which of the following is the graph of $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\frac{1}{2}{\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)}^{x}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186557247 :question/problem "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 5\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}=$" "Given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186557249 :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $Y∼U[0,1]$ be uniformly distributed. Suppose that the function $F(x)$ is a cumulative distribution function, where $F(a)=0,F(b)=1,$ and $F$ is strictly monotonic on $a<x<b.$ Then, the random variable $X,$ given by\n\n$$\nX={F}^{-1}(Y)\n$$\n\nis a continuous random variable with the cumulative distribution function $F(x).$\n\nSo, we need to find the inverse of $y=F(x).$ To do this, we solve the following equation for $x,$ taking into account that $0\\le y\\le 1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({e}^{x}) \\\\ \\frac{y\\pi }{2} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({e}^{x}) \\\\ {e}^{x} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y\\pi }{2}\\right))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the numbers drawn from $U[0,1]$ into the above equation:\n\n- For $y=0.321,$ we get $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{0.321\\pi }{2}\\right))≈-0.595$\n\n- For $y=0.567,$ we get $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{0.567\\pi }{2}\\right))≈0.212$\n\n- For $y=0.804,$ we get $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{0.804\\pi }{2}\\right))≈1.146$" "Let the random variable $Y∼U[0,1]$ be uniformly distributed. Suppose that the function $F(x)$ is a cumulative distribution function, where $F(a)=0,F(b)=1,$ and $F$ is strictly monotonic on $a<x<b.$ Then, the random variable $X,$ given by\n\n$$\nX={F}^{-1}(Y)\n$$\n\nis a continuous random variable with the cumulative distribution function $F(x).$\n\nSo, we need to find the inverse of $y=F(x).$ To do this, we solve the following equation for $x,$ taking into account that $0\\le y\\le 1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({e}^{x}) \\\\ \\frac{y\\pi }{2} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({e}^{x}) \\\\ {e}^{x} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y\\pi }{2}\\right))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the numbers drawn from $U[0,1]$ into the above equation:\n\n- For $y=0.321,$ we get $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{0.321\\pi }{2}\\right))≈-0.595\n$$\n\n- For $y=0.567,$ we get $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{0.567\\pi }{2}\\right))≈0.212\n$$\n\n- For $y=0.804,$ we get $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{0.804\\pi }{2}\\right))≈1.146\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186557250 :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is \n\n$$\n{C}_{3}\\,:\\,3{x}_{2}^{2}={x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis equation defines a parabola that opens in the direction of the positive ${x}_{1}$-axis.\n\nNotice that the other equations do not define a parabola:\n\n- ${C}_{1}\\,:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=0$ defines a single point $(0,0)$ in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane.\n\n- ${C}_{2}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{9}=1$ is the equation of a hyperbola with semiaxes $2$ and $3.$\n\n- ${C}_{4}\\,:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}=4$ represents two parallel lines in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane: ${x}_{1}=2$ and ${x}_{1}=-2.$\n\n- ${C}_{5}\\,:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{4}=-1$ has no points in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane that satisfy the equation (imaginary ellipse)." "The correct answer is \n\n$$\n{C}_{3}\\,:\\,3{x}_{2}^{2}={x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis equation defines a parabola that opens in the direction of the positive ${x}_{1}$-axis.\n\nNotice that the other equations do not define a parabola:\n\n- ${C}_{1}\\,:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=0$ defines a single point $(0,0)$ in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane.\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {C}_{2}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{9}=1\n$$ is the equation of a hyperbola with semiaxes $2$ and $3.$\n\n- ${C}_{4}\\,:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}=4$ represents two parallel lines in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane: ${x}_{1}=2$ and ${x}_{1}=-2.$\n\n- ${C}_{5}\\,:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{4}=-1$ has no points in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane that satisfy the equation (imaginary ellipse)."] [:db/cas 17592186557273 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=-2x+3xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=4y-5xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2x+3xy)=-2+3y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2x+3xy)=3x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4y-5xy)=-5y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4y-5xy)=4-5x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}-2+3y & 3x \\\\ -5y & 4-5x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}-2+3(0) & 3(0) \\\\ -5(0) & 4-5(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=4.$ Since eigenvalues are real with opposite signs, our equilibrium is $\\text{an unstable}$ $\\text{saddle}.$" "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=-2x+3xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=4y-5xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2x+3xy)=-2+3y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2x+3xy)=3x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4y-5xy)=-5y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4y-5xy)=4-5x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}-2+3y & 3x \\\\ -5y & 4-5x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium $$\n\\displaystyle {x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}-2+3(0) & 3(0) \\\\ -5(0) & 4-5(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=4.$ Since eigenvalues are real with opposite signs, our equilibrium is $\\text{an unstable}$ $\\text{saddle}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186557281 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{6t}\\end{aligned}],\\,x(1)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWrite down the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"180px\">$2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1))+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1))$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td><td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,+{\\int }_{1}^{t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"180px\">${e}^{6s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-s))$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,\\text{d}s$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td><td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{6t}\\end{aligned}],\\,x(1)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWrite down the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}],\n$$ where $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"180px\">$2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1))+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1))$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td><td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\displaystyle \\,\\,+{\\int }_{1}^{t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"180px\">${e}^{6s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-s))$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,\\text{d}s$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td><td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [:db/cas 17592186557281 :question/worked-solution "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\underbrace{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}].$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-1)}\\,x(1) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1))+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1)) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1))+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1))\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-s)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-s))\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-s)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-s))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{6s}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{6s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-s)) \\\\ {e}^{6s}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-s))\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1))+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1)) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1))+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1))\\end{aligned}]+{\\int }_{1}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{6s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-s)) \\\\ {e}^{6s}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-s))\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\underbrace{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-1)}\\,x(1) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1))+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1)) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1))+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1))\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-s)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-s))\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-s)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-s))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{6s}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{6s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-s)) \\\\ {e}^{6s}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-s))\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1))+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1)) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1))+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1))\\end{aligned}]+{\\int }_{1}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{6s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-s)) \\\\ {e}^{6s}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-s))\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186557283 :question/problem "Given that\n$M=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $M=LU,$ then $L=$" "Given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle M=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\nhas the LU decomposition $M=LU,$ then $L=$"] [:db/cas 17592186557289 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}],\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWrite down the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} & t{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,+{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">$(t-s){e}^{4t}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,\\text{d}s$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">${e}^{4t}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}],\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWrite down the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} & t{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}],\n$$ where $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\displaystyle \\,\\,+{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">$(t-s){e}^{4t}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,\\text{d}s$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">${e}^{4t}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [:db/cas 17592186557289 :question/worked-solution "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\underbrace{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} & t{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}].$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At}\\,x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} & t{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{4(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4(t-s)}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{4(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4(t-s)}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{4s}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(t-s){e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}]+{\\int }_{0}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}(t-s){e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\underbrace{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} & t{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At}\\,x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} & t{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{4(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4(t-s)}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{4(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4(t-s)}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{4s}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(t-s){e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}]+{\\int }_{0}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}(t-s){e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186557308 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the matrix differential equation $\\,{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}]x(t).$" "Find the general solution to the matrix differential equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\,{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}]x(t).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186557314 :question/problem "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 9\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" "Given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$"] [:db/cas 17592186557325 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWrite down the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,+{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,\\text{d}s$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWrite down the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}],\n$$ where $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\displaystyle \\,\\,+{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,\\text{d}s$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [:db/cas 17592186557325 :question/worked-solution "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\underbrace{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At}\\,x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]+{\\int }_{0}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\underbrace{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At}\\,x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]+{\\int }_{0}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186557331 :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-2y=f(x),\\,y(1)=-4\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is a $2$-periodic function. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(1)=0.$*\n\nThe solution is \n\n$y(x)∼$   \n{{field-1}} \n$+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))$" "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-2y=f(x),\\,y(1)=-4\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is a $2$-periodic function. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(1)=0.$*\n\nThe solution is \n\n$y(x)∼$   \n{{field-1}} \n$$\n\\displaystyle +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186557447 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the following curves and lines.\n$y = \\frac{3}{x}, y = \\frac{7}{x}, y = 4x, y = 5x$" "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the following curves and lines.\n$$\n\\displaystyle y = \\frac{3}{x}, y = \\frac{7}{x}, y = 4x, y = 5x\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186557450 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the following curves and lines.\n$y = \\frac{2}{x}, y = \\frac{5}{x}, y = 2x, y = 3x$" "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the following curves and lines.\n$$\n\\displaystyle y = \\frac{2}{x}, y = \\frac{5}{x}, y = 2x, y = 3x\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186557458 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the following curves and lines.\n$y = \\frac{1}{x}, y = \\frac{4}{x}, y = 3x, y = 4x$" "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the following curves and lines.\n$$\n\\displaystyle y = \\frac{1}{x}, y = \\frac{4}{x}, y = 3x, y = 4x\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186557473 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the following curves and lines.\n$y = \\frac{4}{x}, y = \\frac{5}{x}, y = 3x, y = 5x$" "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the following curves and lines.\n$$\n\\displaystyle y = \\frac{4}{x}, y = \\frac{5}{x}, y = 3x, y = 5x\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186557489 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the following curves and lines.\n$y = \\frac{3}{x}, y = \\frac{7}{x}, y = 3x, y = 5x$" "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the following curves and lines.\n$$\n\\displaystyle y = \\frac{3}{x}, y = \\frac{7}{x}, y = 3x, y = 5x\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186557577 :question/worked-solution "The feed concentration must use kilograms: $60\\,\\text{g/L}\\cdot\\frac{1\\,\\text{kg}}{1000\\,\\text{g}}=0.06\\,\\text{kg/L}.$\n\nLet $Q$ be the amount of salt in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the tank minus the rate at which salt leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.06\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the salt enters the tank at a rate of $40\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.06\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (40\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.06\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (40\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =2.4\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $560\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{560}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $40\\,\\text{L/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(40\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{560}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(40\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{560}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{40Q}{560}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{14}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =2.4-\\frac{Q}{14}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{14}Q=2.4.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{14}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/14},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{14}\\alpha =2.4\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =33.6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=33.6.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/14}+33.6.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $16\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=16,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =16 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/14}+33.6 & =16 \\\\ A & =-17.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=33.6-17.6{e}^{-t/14}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of salt in the tank $2$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\nQ(2)=33.6-17.6{e}^{-2/14}≈18.34\\,\\text{kg},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." "The feed concentration must use kilograms: $$\n\\displaystyle 60\\,\\text{g/L}\\cdot\\frac{1\\,\\text{kg}}{1000\\,\\text{g}}=0.06\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nLet $Q$ be the amount of salt in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the tank minus the rate at which salt leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.06\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the salt enters the tank at a rate of $40\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.06\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (40\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.06\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (40\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =2.4\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $560\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{560}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $40\\,\\text{L/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(40\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{560}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(40\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{560}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{40Q}{560}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{14}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =2.4-\\frac{Q}{14}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{14}Q=2.4.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{14}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/14},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{14}\\alpha =2.4\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =33.6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=33.6.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/14}+33.6.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $16\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=16,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =16 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/14}+33.6 & =16 \\\\ A & =-17.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=33.6-17.6{e}^{-t/14}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of salt in the tank $2$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\nQ(2)=33.6-17.6{e}^{-2/14}≈18.34\\,\\text{kg},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186557580 :question/worked-solution "The feed concentration must use kilograms: $60\\,\\text{g/L}\\cdot\\frac{1\\,\\text{kg}}{1000\\,\\text{g}}=0.06\\,\\text{kg/L}.$\n\nLet $Q$ be the amount of dye in the vat at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which dye enters the vat minus the rate at which dye leaves the vat.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which dye enters the vat.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.06\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the dye enters the vat at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *dye* enters the vat is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.06\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.06\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.9\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which dye exits the vat.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the vat remains $900\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of dye in the vat, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{900}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min},$ *dye* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(15\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{900}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{900}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{15Q}{900}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{60}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.9-\\frac{Q}{60}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{60}Q=0.9.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{60}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/60},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{60}\\alpha =0.9\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =54.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=54.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/60}+54.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the vat initially contains $15\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved dye. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=15,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =15 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/60}+54 & =15 \\\\ A & =-39\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=54-39{e}^{-t/60}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of dye in the vat $5$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\nQ(5)=54-39{e}^{-5/60}≈18.12\\,\\text{kg},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." "The feed concentration must use kilograms: $$\n\\displaystyle 60\\,\\text{g/L}\\cdot\\frac{1\\,\\text{kg}}{1000\\,\\text{g}}=0.06\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nLet $Q$ be the amount of dye in the vat at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which dye enters the vat minus the rate at which dye leaves the vat.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which dye enters the vat.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.06\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the dye enters the vat at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, 