\left(- x y^{2} - x\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y,\n$$ where $C$ is part of the ellipse $x^{2} + 9 y^{2}=9$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $(3,0)$ to the point $(0,1)$."] [:db/cas 17592186557837 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\int_C\\left(- 4 x + y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(- x + 3 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y,$ where $C$ is part of the ellipse $x^{2} + 9 y^{2}=9$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $(3,0)$ to the point $(0,1)$." "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C\\left(- 4 x + y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(- x + 3 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y,\n$$ where $C$ is part of the ellipse $x^{2} + 9 y^{2}=9$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $(3,0)$ to the point $(0,1)$."] [:db/cas 17592186558529 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,\\underbrace{(LU)}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL\\underbrace{(Ux)}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -5 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=-1 \\\\ -2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=-3 \\\\ 8{y}_{1}-5{y}_{2}+{y}_{3}=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the solution to this system using forward substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =-1, \\\\ {y}_{2} & =-3+2{y}_{1} \\\\  & =-3+2(-1) \\\\  & =-5, \\\\ {y}_{3} & =8-8{y}_{1}+5{y}_{2} \\\\  & =8-8(-1)+5(-5) \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$y=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}].$" "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,\\underbrace{(LU)}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL\\underbrace{(Ux)}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -5 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=-1 \\\\ -2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=-3 \\\\ 8{y}_{1}-5{y}_{2}+{y}_{3}=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the solution to this system using forward substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =-1, \\\\ {y}_{2} & =-3+2{y}_{1} \\\\  & =-3+2(-1) \\\\  & =-5, \\\\ {y}_{3} & =8-8{y}_{1}+5{y}_{2} \\\\  & =8-8(-1)+5(-5) \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$$\n\\displaystyle y=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186558544 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x(1)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWrite down the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} & t{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">$(t-2){e}^{3(t-1)}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,+{\\int }_{1}^{t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,\\text{d}s$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x(1)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWrite down the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} & t{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}],\n$$ where $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">$(t-2){e}^{3(t-1)}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\displaystyle \\,\\,+{\\int }_{1}^{t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,\\text{d}s$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [:db/cas 17592186558544 :question/worked-solution "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\underbrace{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} & t{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}].$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-1)}\\,x(1) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3(t-1)} & (t-1){e}^{3(t-1)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3(t-1)}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(t-2){e}^{3(t-1)} \\\\ {e}^{3(t-1)}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{3(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3(t-s)}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{3(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3(t-s)}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3s} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}(t-2){e}^{3(t-1)} \\\\ {e}^{3(t-1)}\\end{aligned}]+{\\int }_{1}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\underbrace{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} & t{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-1)}\\,x(1) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3(t-1)} & (t-1){e}^{3(t-1)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3(t-1)}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(t-2){e}^{3(t-1)} \\\\ {e}^{3(t-1)}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{3(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3(t-s)}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{3(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3(t-s)}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3s} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}(t-2){e}^{3(t-1)} \\\\ {e}^{3(t-1)}\\end{aligned}]+{\\int }_{1}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186558563 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWrite down the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"120px\">$\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t)+2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td><td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,+{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"120px\">{{field-1}} </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,\\text{d}s$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td><td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">${e}^{s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t-s)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWrite down the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}],\n$$ where $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"120px\">$\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t)+2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td><td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\displaystyle \\,\\,+{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"120px\">{{field-1}} </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,\\text{d}s$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td><td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">${e}^{s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t-s)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [:db/cas 17592186558563 :question/worked-solution "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\underbrace{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At}\\,x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t)+2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t)+2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t-s) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t-s) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{s} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{s}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t-s) \\\\ {e}^{s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t)+2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t)+2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}]+{\\int }_{0}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{s}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t-s) \\\\ {e}^{s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\underbrace{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At}\\,x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t)+2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t)+2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t-s) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t-s) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{s} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{s}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t-s) \\\\ {e}^{s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t)+2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t)+2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}]+{\\int }_{0}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{s}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t-s) \\\\ {e}^{s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186558932 :question/problem "![](images/9e/9e863fbe1fbcc931a1acaf67fe92990d4bd0551f8c5a1a89a01c8eeba367d668.png)\n\nGiven $\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=- 2 x^{2} - 2 y^{2}$, calculate $\\oint_{\\partial D}P\\,\\mathrm dx+Q\\,\\mathrm dy$ over the positively oriented annular region shown above." "![](images/9e/9e863fbe1fbcc931a1acaf67fe92990d4bd0551f8c5a1a89a01c8eeba367d668.png)\n\nGiven $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=- 2 x^{2} - 2 y^{2},\n$$ calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_{\\partial D}P\\,\\mathrm dx+Q\\,\\mathrm dy\n$$ over the positively oriented annular region shown above."] [:db/cas 17592186558943 :question/problem "![](images/c4/c4b3ac630020f1c4bd2d19dfb311b00fc0af41876497ce4772838e90ced60bd7.png)\n\nGiven $\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=- 2 x^{2} - 2 y^{2}$, calculate $\\oint_{\\partial D}P\\,\\mathrm dx+Q\\,\\mathrm dy$ over the positively oriented annular region shown above." "![](images/c4/c4b3ac630020f1c4bd2d19dfb311b00fc0af41876497ce4772838e90ced60bd7.png)\n\nGiven $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=- 2 x^{2} - 2 y^{2},\n$$ calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_{\\partial D}P\\,\\mathrm dx+Q\\,\\mathrm dy\n$$ over the positively oriented annular region shown above."] [:db/cas 17592186558949 :question/problem "![](images/37/37c3ffbceecd99f2c618c1401e392b27620a0e34ce284683446eae4217c5e973.png)\n\nGiven $\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=3 x^{2} + 3 y^{2}$, calculate $\\oint_{\\partial D}P\\,\\mathrm dx+Q\\,\\mathrm dy$ over the positively oriented annular region shown above." "![](images/37/37c3ffbceecd99f2c618c1401e392b27620a0e34ce284683446eae4217c5e973.png)\n\nGiven $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=3 x^{2} + 3 y^{2},\n$$ calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_{\\partial D}P\\,\\mathrm dx+Q\\,\\mathrm dy\n$$ over the positively oriented annular region shown above."] [:db/cas 17592186558956 :question/problem "![](images/42/424cfad485da7dd6cac42c9784df348a2a09d2bb4da651926cae32aa6e06cded.png)\n\nGiven $\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=2 x^{2} + 2 y^{2}$, calculate $\\oint_{\\partial D}P\\,\\mathrm dx+Q\\,\\mathrm dy$ over the positively oriented annular region shown above." "![](images/42/424cfad485da7dd6cac42c9784df348a2a09d2bb4da651926cae32aa6e06cded.png)\n\nGiven $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=2 x^{2} + 2 y^{2},\n$$ calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_{\\partial D}P\\,\\mathrm dx+Q\\,\\mathrm dy\n$$ over the positively oriented annular region shown above."] [:db/cas 17592186558965 :question/problem "![](images/b7/b73bdd66e1e9ac758c98d854bfe93b6fcf82ff8d85322f607e50b75a5797770f.png)\n\nGiven $\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=x^{2} + y^{2}$, calculate $\\oint_{\\partial D}P\\,\\mathrm dx+Q\\,\\mathrm dy$ over the positively oriented annular region shown above." "![](images/b7/b73bdd66e1e9ac758c98d854bfe93b6fcf82ff8d85322f607e50b75a5797770f.png)\n\nGiven $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=x^{2} + y^{2},\n$$ calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_{\\partial D}P\\,\\mathrm dx+Q\\,\\mathrm dy\n$$ over the positively oriented annular region shown above."] [:db/cas 17592186558967 :question/problem "![](images/c4/c4b3ac630020f1c4bd2d19dfb311b00fc0af41876497ce4772838e90ced60bd7.png)\n\nCalculate $\\oint_{\\partial D}(x^{2} y + y^{3})\\,\\mathrm dx+(- x^{3} - x y^{2})\\,\\mathrm dy$, where $D$ is the annular region shown above." "![](images/c4/c4b3ac630020f1c4bd2d19dfb311b00fc0af41876497ce4772838e90ced60bd7.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_{\\partial D}(x^{2} y + y^{3})\\,\\mathrm dx+(- x^{3} - x y^{2})\\,\\mathrm dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above."] [:db/cas 17592186559788 :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\n{B}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{B}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\},$ and $[A]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$" "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\n{B}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{B}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\},$ and $$\n\\displaystyle [A]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186559788 :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{B}_{1}+{x}_{2}{B}_{2}=A.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} & 2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ 0 & 3{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=-10 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & -10 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 10\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & -10 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 10\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -20 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -20\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=-10 \\\\ 5{x}_{2}=-20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second  equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-4.$ From the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+2(-4) & =-10 \\\\ -{x}_{1} & =-2 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $[A]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=2+(-4)=-2.$" "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{B}_{1}+{x}_{2}{B}_{2}=A.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} & 2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ 0 & 3{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=-10 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & -10 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 10\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & -10 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 10\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -20 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -20\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=-10 \\\\ 5{x}_{2}=-20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second  equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-4.$ From the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+2(-4) & =-10 \\\\ -{x}_{1} & =-2 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle [A]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=2+(-4)=-2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186559789 :question/problem "Let $b=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Find $-\\frac{1}{2}b.$" "Let $$\n\\displaystyle b=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$ Find $-\\frac{1}{2}b.$"] [:db/cas 17592186559791 :question/problem "Let $a=[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].$ Find $2a.$" "Let $$\n\\displaystyle a=[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$ Find $2a.$"] [:db/cas 17592186559795 :question/problem "Let\n$r=[\\begin{aligned}x-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $s=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ x \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\nIf $r\\cdot s=7$, find the value of $x.$" "Let\n$$\n\\displaystyle r=[\\begin{aligned}x-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle s=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ x \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\nIf $r\\cdot s=7$, find the value of $x.$"] [:db/cas 17592186559803 :question/problem "Let $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $c=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].$ Find $b+c.$" "Let $$\n\\displaystyle b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle c=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].\n$$ Find $b+c.$"] [:db/cas 17592186559803 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our vectors:\n\n- The vector $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ has $5$ components as it lies in ${R}^{5}.$\n\n- The vector $c=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]$ has $4$ components as it lies in ${R}^{4}.$\n\nTherefore, since the number of components does not match, the operation is undefined." "Let's examine our vectors:\n\n- The vector $$\n\\displaystyle b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$ has $5$ components as it lies in ${R}^{5}.$\n\n- The vector $$\n\\displaystyle c=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$ has $4$ components as it lies in ${R}^{4}.$\n\nTherefore, since the number of components does not match, the operation is undefined."] [:db/cas 17592186559815 :question/problem "Consider the polynomials ${p}_{1}(t)$ and ${p}_{2}(t),$ given by\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=t-{t}^{3},\\,{p}_{2}(t)=2{t}^{2}+5{t}^{3}.\n$$\n\nFind $p(t)$ given that $B=\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{p}_{1},{p}_{2}\\},$ and the coordinates of $p(t)$ relative to $B$ are $[p(t)]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" "Consider the polynomials ${p}_{1}(t)$ and ${p}_{2}(t),$ given by\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=t-{t}^{3},\\,{p}_{2}(t)=2{t}^{2}+5{t}^{3}.\n$$\n\nFind $p(t)$ given that $B=\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{p}_{1},{p}_{2}\\},$ and the coordinates of $p(t)$ relative to $B$ are $$\n\\displaystyle [p(t)]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186559818 :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4y-2x-3 \\\\ {x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+3y-3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+4y-2x-3)=2x-2,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+4y-2x-3)=4 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+3y-3)=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+3y-3)=-2y+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}2x-2 & 4 \\\\ 2x & -2y+3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}2(1)-2 & 4 \\\\ 2(1) & -2(1)+3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, near the equilibrium, the nonlinear system is approximated by\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]u,\n$$\n\nwhere the deviation vector is\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$ and \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4y-2x-3 \\\\ {x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+3y-3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+4y-2x-3)=2x-2,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+4y-2x-3)=4 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+3y-3)=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+3y-3)=-2y+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}2x-2 & 4 \\\\ 2x & -2y+3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium $$\n\\displaystyle {x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}2(1)-2 & 4 \\\\ 2(1) & -2(1)+3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, near the equilibrium, the nonlinear system is approximated by\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]u,\n$$\n\nwhere the deviation vector is\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186559834 :question/problem "Let $r=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ x\\end{aligned}]$ and $s=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ {x}^{4} \\\\ 8 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$\nIf $r\\cdot s=0$ and $x<0$, find the value of $x.$" "Let $$\n\\displaystyle r=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ x\\end{aligned}]\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle s=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ {x}^{4} \\\\ 8 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\nIf $r\\cdot s=0$ and $x<0$, find the value of $x.$"] [:db/cas 17592186559836 :question/problem "Let $r=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $s=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 12 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ What is $r\\cdot s?$" "Let $$\n\\displaystyle r=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle s=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 12 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$ What is $r\\cdot s?$"] [:db/cas 17592186559836 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our vectors:\n\n- The vector $r=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has $4$ components as it lies in ${R}^{4}.$\n\n- The vector $s=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 12 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ has $5$ components as it lies in ${R}^{5}.$\n\nTherefore, since the number of components does not match, the operation is undefined." "Let's examine our vectors:\n\n- The vector $$\n\\displaystyle r=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$ has $4$ components as it lies in ${R}^{4}.$\n\n- The vector $$\n\\displaystyle s=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 12 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$ has $5$ components as it lies in ${R}^{5}.$\n\nTherefore, since the number of components does not match, the operation is undefined."] [:db/cas 17592186559840 :question/problem "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ 4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ k+2\\end{aligned}].$ If $u\\cdot v=0$, find the value of $k.$" "Let $$\n\\displaystyle u=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle v=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ 4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ k+2\\end{aligned}].\n$$ If $u\\cdot v=0$, find the value of $k.$"] [:db/cas 17592186559845 :question/problem "Let $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}].$ Find $2v+\\frac{1}{4}w.$" "Let $$\n\\displaystyle v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle w=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}].\n$$ Find $2v+\\frac{1}{4}w.$"] [:db/cas 17592186559845 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our vectors:\n\n- The vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has $5$ components as it lies in ${R}^{5}.$\n\n- The vector $w=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}]$ has $4$ components as it lies in ${R}^{4}.$\n\nTherefore, since the number of components does not match, the operation is undefined." "Let's examine our vectors:\n\n- The vector $$\n\\displaystyle v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$ has $5$ components as it lies in ${R}^{5}.$\n\n- The vector $$\n\\displaystyle w=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}]\n$$ has $4$ components as it lies in ${R}^{4}.$\n\nTherefore, since the number of components does not match, the operation is undefined."] [:db/cas 17592186559846 :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}y\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\nJ=|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+{y}^{2})=\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x),\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+{y}^{2})=2y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y)=2xy,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y)={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nJ=|\\begin{aligned}\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x) & 2y \\\\ 2xy & {x}^{2}\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J(0,0) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) & 2(0) \\\\ 2(0)(0) & {0}^{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\pi  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot 0-0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the Jacobian determinant is zero, our system is $\\text{not almost linear}$ at ${x}^{∗}.$" "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$ and \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\nJ=|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+{y}^{2})=\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x),\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+{y}^{2})=2y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y)=2xy,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y)={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nJ=|\\begin{aligned}\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x) & 2y \\\\ 2xy & {x}^{2}\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium $$\n\\displaystyle {x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J(0,0) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) & 2(0) \\\\ 2(0)(0) & {0}^{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\pi  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot 0-0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the Jacobian determinant is zero, our system is $\\text{not almost linear}$ at ${x}^{∗}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186559855 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. Any $5\\times 3$ matrix $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values.\n\nII. Any $3\\times 5$ matrix $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values.\n\nIII. The singular values of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ are negative." "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. Any $5\\times 3$ matrix $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values.\n\nII. Any $3\\times 5$ matrix $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values.\n\nIII. The singular values of the matrix $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$ are negative."] [:db/cas 17592186559862 :question/problem "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Find $u+2v.$" "Let $$\n\\displaystyle u=[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$ Find $u+2v.$"] [:db/cas 17592186559862 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our vectors:\n\n- The vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has $3$ components as it lies in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has $4$ components as it lies in ${R}^{4}.$\n\nTherefore, since the number of components does not match, the operation is undefined." "Let's examine our vectors:\n\n- The vector $$\n\\displaystyle u=[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$ has $3$ components as it lies in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The vector $$\n\\displaystyle v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$ has $4$ components as it lies in ${R}^{4}.$\n\nTherefore, since the number of components does not match, the operation is undefined."] [:db/cas 17592186559863 :question/problem "Let $c=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 20 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].$ Find $\\frac{1}{4}c.$" "Let $$\n\\displaystyle c=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 20 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].\n$$ Find $\\frac{1}{4}c.$"] [:db/cas 17592186559868 :question/problem "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]$, then $-2v=$" "If $$\n\\displaystyle v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}],\n$$ then $-2v=$"] [:db/cas 17592186559870 :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\n{B}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{B}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 12\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\},$ and $[A]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$" "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\n{B}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{B}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 12\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\},$ and $$\n\\displaystyle [A]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186559870 :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{B}_{1}+{x}_{2}{B}_{2}=A.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} & -2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ 0 & 3{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=-2 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-6 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 12\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 12\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -10 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 18\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=-2 \\\\ 5{x}_{2}=-10 \\\\ -9{x}_{2}=18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third equations, we get ${x}_{2}=-2.$ From the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2(-2) & =-2 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $[A]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=2+(-2)=0.$" "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{B}_{1}+{x}_{2}{B}_{2}=A.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} & -2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ 0 & 3{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=-2 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-6 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 12\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 12\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -10 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 18\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=-2 \\\\ 5{x}_{2}=-10 \\\\ -9{x}_{2}=18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third equations, we get ${x}_{2}=-2.$ From the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2(-2) & =-2 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle [A]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=2+(-2)=0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186559879 :question/problem "Let\n$v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ p \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ p\\end{aligned}].$\nIf $v\\cdot w=3$, find the value of $p.$" "Let\n$$\n\\displaystyle v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ p \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle w=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ p\\end{aligned}].\n$$\nIf $v\\cdot w=3$, find the value of $p.$"] [:db/cas 17592186559880 :question/problem "$$\n{A}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{A}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Find the matrix $C$ given that $B=\\{{A}_{1},{A}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{A}_{1},{A}_{2}\\},$ and the coordinates of the matrix $C$ relative to $B$ are $[C]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$" "$$\n{A}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{A}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Find the matrix $C$ given that $B=\\{{A}_{1},{A}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{A}_{1},{A}_{2}\\},$ and the coordinates of the matrix $C$ relative to $B$ are $$\n\\displaystyle [C]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186559885 :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+xy-6 \\\\ -{y}^{2}+2x-3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+xy-6)=2x+y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+xy-6)=x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{y}^{2}+2x-3)=2 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{y}^{2}+2x-3)=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}2x+y & x \\\\ 2 & -2y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}2(2)+1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2(1)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), so the system is almost linear at ${x}^{∗}.$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )(-2-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -6 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=\\frac{3}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{33}}{2}.$\n\nSince the eigenvalues are real and have opposite signs, our equilibrium is an $\\text{unstable}$ $\\text{saddle}.$" "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$ and \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+xy-6 \\\\ -{y}^{2}+2x-3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+xy-6)=2x+y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+xy-6)=x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{y}^{2}+2x-3)=2 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{y}^{2}+2x-3)=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}2x+y & x \\\\ 2 & -2y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium $$\n\\displaystyle {x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}2(2)+1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2(1)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), so the system is almost linear at ${x}^{∗}.$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )(-2-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -6 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues $$\n\\displaystyle {\\lambda }_{1,2}=\\frac{3}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{33}}{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the eigenvalues are real and have opposite signs, our equilibrium is an $\\text{unstable}$ $\\text{saddle}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186559891 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ has two negative singular values.\n\nII. Any $4\\times 5$ matrix $A$ has at most $5$ distinct singular values.\n\nIII. Any $8\\times 4$ matrix $A$ has at most $8$ distinct singular values." "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The matrix $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$ has two negative singular values.\n\nII. Any $4\\times 5$ matrix $A$ has at most $5$ distinct singular values.\n\nIII. Any $8\\times 4$ matrix $A$ has at most $8$ distinct singular values."] [:db/cas 17592186560130 :question/problem "Which integral is obtained from $\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ given that $F(x,y)=(- 3 y^{2})\\,i+(- 3 x^{2})\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(t^{2})\\,i+(t^{4})\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,1]$? Use the supplied parameterization and limits to compute $F(r(t))\\cdot r'(t)$. Do not evaluate the integral or change variables." "Which integral is obtained from $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y)=(- 3 y^{2})\\,i+(- 3 x^{2})\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(t^{2})\\,i+(t^{4})\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,1]$? Use the supplied parameterization and limits to compute $F(r(t))\\cdot r'(t)$. Do not evaluate the integral or change variables."] [:db/cas 17592186560131 :question/problem "Which integral is obtained from $\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ given that $F(x,y)=(y^{2})\\,i+(- x^{2})\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(t^{3})\\,i+(t^{2})\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,1]$? Use the supplied parameterization and limits to compute $F(r(t))\\cdot r'(t)$. Do not evaluate the integral or change variables." "Which integral is obtained from $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y)=(y^{2})\\,i+(- x^{2})\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(t^{3})\\,i+(t^{2})\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,1]$? Use the supplied parameterization and limits to compute $F(r(t))\\cdot r'(t)$. Do not evaluate the integral or change variables."] [:db/cas 17592186560133 :question/problem "Which integral is obtained from $\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ given that $F(x,y)=(- 2 y^{2})\\,i+(- 4 x^{2})\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(t^{2})\\,i+(t^{3})\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,3]$? Use the supplied parameterization and limits to compute $F(r(t))\\cdot r'(t)$. Do not evaluate the integral or change variables." "Which integral is obtained from $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y)=(- 2 y^{2})\\,i+(- 4 x^{2})\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(t^{2})\\,i+(t^{3})\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,3]$? Use the supplied parameterization and limits to compute $F(r(t))\\cdot r'(t)$. Do not evaluate the integral or change variables."] [:db/cas 17592186560151 :question/problem "Which integral is obtained from $\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ given that $F(x,y)=(- x - 4 y)\\,i+(x - 3 y)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi}{3}]$? Use the supplied parameterization and limits to compute $F(r(t))\\cdot r'(t)$. Do not evaluate the integral or change variables." "Which integral is obtained from $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y)=(- x - 4 y)\\,i+(x - 3 y)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi}{3}]$? Use the supplied parameterization and limits to compute $