tegral is obtained from $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y)=(x + y)\\,i+(- 4 x + 3 y)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}]$? Use the supplied parameterization and limits to compute $F(r(t))\\cdot r'(t)$. Do not evaluate the integral or change variables."] [:db/cas 17592186560177 :question/problem "Which integral is obtained from $\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ given that $F(x,y)=(y^{2})\\,i+(- 2 x^{2})\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(t^{4})\\,i+(t^{3})\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,3]$? Use the supplied parameterization and limits to compute $F(r(t))\\cdot r'(t)$. Do not evaluate the integral or change variables." "Which integral is obtained from $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y)=(y^{2})\\,i+(- 2 x^{2})\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(t^{4})\\,i+(t^{3})\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,3]$? Use the supplied parameterization and limits to compute $F(r(t))\\cdot r'(t)$. Do not evaluate the integral or change variables."] [:db/cas 17592186560183 :question/problem "Which integral is obtained from $\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ given that $F(x,y)=(y)\\,i+(- x)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(\\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}]$? Use the supplied parameterization and limits to compute $F(r(t))\\cdot r'(t)$. Do not evaluate the integral or change variables." "Which integral is obtained from $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y)=(y)\\,i+(- x)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(\\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}]$? Use the supplied parameterization and limits to compute $F(r(t))\\cdot r'(t)$. Do not evaluate the integral or change variables."] [:db/cas 17592186560194 :question/problem "Which integral is obtained from $\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ given that $F(x,y)=(- y^{2})\\,i+(4 x^{2})\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(t^{2})\\,i+(t^{3})\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2]$? Use the supplied parameterization and limits to compute $F(r(t))\\cdot r'(t)$. Do not evaluate the integral or change variables." "Which integral is obtained from $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y)=(- y^{2})\\,i+(4 x^{2})\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(t^{2})\\,i+(t^{3})\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2]$? Use the supplied parameterization and limits to compute $F(r(t))\\cdot r'(t)$. Do not evaluate the integral or change variables."] [:db/cas 17592186560198 :question/problem "Which integral is obtained from $\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ given that $F(x,y)=(x + 2 y)\\,i+(x + 3 y)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{4 \\pi}{3}]$? Use the supplied parameterization and limits to compute $F(r(t))\\cdot r'(t)$. Do not evaluate the integral or change variables." "Which integral is obtained from $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y)=(x + 2 y)\\,i+(x + 3 y)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{4 \\pi}{3}]$? Use the supplied parameterization and limits to compute $F(r(t))\\cdot r'(t)$. Do not evaluate the integral or change variables."] [:db/cas 17592186560215 :question/problem "Which integral is obtained from $\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ given that $F(x,y)=(x y^{2})\\,i+(2 y)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(e^{t})\\,i+(\\sqrt{t})\\,j$ for $t\\in [1,3]$? Use the supplied parameterization and limits to compute $F(r(t))\\cdot r'(t)$. Do not evaluate the integral or change variables." "Which integral is obtained from $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y)=(x y^{2})\\,i+(2 y)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(e^{t})\\,i+(\\sqrt{t})\\,j$ for $t\\in [1,3]$? Use the supplied parameterization and limits to compute $F(r(t))\\cdot r'(t)$. Do not evaluate the integral or change variables."] [:db/cas 17592186560219 :question/problem "Which integral is obtained from $\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ given that $F(x,y)=(- 2 x + 4 y)\\,i+(2 x + 3 y)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi}{3}]$? Use the supplied parameterization and limits to compute $F(r(t))\\cdot r'(t)$. Do not evaluate the integral or change variables." "Which integral is obtained from $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y)=(- 2 x + 4 y)\\,i+(2 x + 3 y)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi}{3}]$? Use the supplied parameterization and limits to compute $F(r(t))\\cdot r'(t)$. Do not evaluate the integral or change variables."] [:db/cas 17592186560971 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ may be written as a composition of Jordan blocks, as follows:\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{2}(1)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(-3)\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n- a single $2\\times 2$ Jordan block corresponding to $\\lambda =1,$ and\n\n- a single $1\\times 1$ block corresponding to $\\lambda =-3.$\n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chains: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(1) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0 &  \\\\ {J}_{1}(-3) & : & {v}_{3}\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that \n\n- the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1,$\n\n- the second column of $P$ corresponds to the vector ${v}_{2}$ such that $(A-I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}.$\n\nNow, computing $A-I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 11 & -3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 11 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(A-I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 7 & 11 & -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-7){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -4 & 8\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we have ${x}_{2}={x}_{3}+2.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+({x}_{3}+2)=-1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-{x}_{3}-3.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{3}-3 \\\\ {x}_{3}+2 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{3}=0,$ we get  \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $b-c=2-0=2.$" "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ may be written as a composition of Jordan blocks, as follows:\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{2}(1)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(-3)\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n- a single $2\\times 2$ Jordan block corresponding to $\\lambda =1,$ and\n\n- a single $1\\times 1$ block corresponding to $\\lambda =-3.$\n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chains: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(1) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0 &  \\\\ {J}_{1}(-3) & : & {v}_{3}\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that \n\n- the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$ of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1,$\n\n- the second column of $P$ corresponds to the vector ${v}_{2}$ such that $(A-I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}.$\n\nNow, computing $A-I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 11 & -3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 11 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(A-I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 7 & 11 & -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-7){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -4 & 8\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we have ${x}_{2}={x}_{3}+2.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+({x}_{3}+2)=-1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-{x}_{3}-3.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{3}-3 \\\\ {x}_{3}+2 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{3}=0,$ we get  \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $b-c=2-0=2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186560984 :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the vector field $F(x,y)?$\n\nI. $F(x,y)=⟨{e}^{y}-y,\\,x{e}^{y}⟩$\n\nII. $F(x,y)=⟨{e}^{y},\\,0⟩$\n\nIII. $F(x,y)=⟨-\\frac{y}{5}+{x}^{7},\\,\\frac{6x}{5}⟩$" "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the vector field $F(x,y)?$\n\nI. $F(x,y)=⟨{e}^{y}-y,\\,x{e}^{y}⟩$\n\nII. $F(x,y)=⟨{e}^{y},\\,0⟩$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle F(x,y)=⟨-\\frac{y}{5}+{x}^{7},\\,\\frac{6x}{5}⟩\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186560990 :question/problem "Which of the following are rotation-scaling matrices?\n\nI. $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$\n\nII. $B=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -4\\end{aligned}]$\n\nIII. $C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" "Which of the following are rotation-scaling matrices?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle B=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186560990 :question/worked-solution "A rotation-scaling matrix has the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & -b \\\\ b & a\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n where $a$ and $b$ are real numbers that are not equal to zero simultaneously. Notice that the entries on the main diagonal are the same, while the antidiagonal entries have opposite signs.\n\nWith that in mind let's check each of the given matrices:\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$  is *not* a rotation-scaling matrix since all entries are zero.\n\n- $B=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -4\\end{aligned}]$ is *not* a rotation-scaling matrix since the entries on the main diagonal are not the same.\n\n- $C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ is *not* a rotation-scaling matrix since the entries on the antidiagonal do not have opposite signs.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"None.\"" "A rotation-scaling matrix has the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & -b \\\\ b & a\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n where $a$ and $b$ are real numbers that are not equal to zero simultaneously. Notice that the entries on the main diagonal are the same, while the antidiagonal entries have opposite signs.\n\nWith that in mind let's check each of the given matrices:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$  is *not* a rotation-scaling matrix since all entries are zero.\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle B=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$ is *not* a rotation-scaling matrix since the entries on the main diagonal are not the same.\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$ is *not* a rotation-scaling matrix since the entries on the antidiagonal do not have opposite signs.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"None.\""] [:db/cas 17592186560992 :question/problem "Which of the following are rotation-scaling matrices?\n\nI. $A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 9 \\\\ -9 & 5\\end{aligned}]$\n\nII. $B=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nIII. $C=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 9\\end{aligned}]$" "Which of the following are rotation-scaling matrices?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 9 \\\\ -9 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle B=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle C=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186560992 :question/worked-solution "A rotation-scaling matrix has the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & -b \\\\ b & a\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n where $a$ and $b$ are real numbers that are not equal to zero simultaneously. Notice that the entries on the main diagonal are the same, while the antidiagonal entries have opposite signs.\n\nWith that in mind let's check each of the given matrices:\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 9 \\\\ -9 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ is a rotation-scaling matrix.\n\n- $B=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]$ is a rotation-scaling matrix.\n\n- $C=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 9\\end{aligned}]$ is *not* a rotation-scaling matrix since the entries on the antidiagonal do not have opposite signs.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "A rotation-scaling matrix has the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & -b \\\\ b & a\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n where $a$ and $b$ are real numbers that are not equal to zero simultaneously. Notice that the entries on the main diagonal are the same, while the antidiagonal entries have opposite signs.\n\nWith that in mind let's check each of the given matrices:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 9 \\\\ -9 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$ is a rotation-scaling matrix.\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle B=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$ is a rotation-scaling matrix.\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle C=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$ is *not* a rotation-scaling matrix since the entries on the antidiagonal do not have opposite signs.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186560997 :question/problem "A linear transformation $T$ represents a dilation.  Given that $v=[\\begin{aligned}-35 \\\\ -25\\end{aligned}]$ and $T(v)=[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],$ what is the standard matrix of $T?$" "A linear transformation $T$ represents a dilation.  Given that $$\n\\displaystyle v=[\\begin{aligned}-35 \\\\ -25\\end{aligned}]\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle T(v)=[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],\n$$ what is the standard matrix of $T?$"] [:db/cas 17592186560998 :question/worked-solution "Given a matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda ,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $k$ is a vector $v$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)^{k}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-\\lambda I)^{k-1}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTo find a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to $\\lambda =7,$ we need to find a vector $v$ that satisfies \n\n$$\n(A-7I)^{2}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-7I)v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each condition:\n\n- We start by solving $(A-7I)^{2}v=0.$ First, we have\n\n$$\nA-7I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nComputing its square, we see that this matrix is nilpotent:\n\n$$\n(A-7I)^{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis means that *any* vector in ${R}^{2}$ is a solution to $(A-7I)^{2}v=0.$ Therefore, the solution to $(A-7I)^{2}v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2}\\end{aligned}]={v}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{v}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $(A-7I)v=0.$ We have\n\n$$\nA-7I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,.\n$$\n\nConverting this matrix to reduced row echelon form, we get\n\n$$\nA-7I∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis means that the solution to $(A-7I)v=0$ is ${v}_{2}=0,$ where ${v}_{1}$ is a free variable. Therefore, the solution to $(A-7I)v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={v}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing our two solutions, we see that the vector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ satisfies $(A-7I)^{2}v=0$ but does *not* satisfy $(A-7I)v=0.$  \n\nHence, $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ of $A.$" "Given a matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda ,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $k$ is a vector $v$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)^{k}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-\\lambda I)^{k-1}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTo find a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to $\\lambda =7,$ we need to find a vector $v$ that satisfies \n\n$$\n(A-7I)^{2}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-7I)v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nLet's conside