c{y}{8} + 3 e^{3 x + 3 y},\\,\\frac{9 x}{8} + 3 e^{3 x + 3 y}⟩\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186561531 :question/worked-solution "According to Green's theorem, if $F(x,y)=⟨P(x,y),\\,Q(x,y)⟩,$ where\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Vector field I gives the required area. In this case,\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=9 y^{2} - \\frac{1}{8}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=9 y^{2} + \\frac{7}{8},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(9 y^{2} + \\frac{7}{8}\\right)-\\left(9 y^{2} - \\frac{1}{8}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)+\\left(9 y^{2}\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{1}{8}\\right)-\\left(9 y^{2}\\right)=1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II does *not* give the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3 e^{3 x}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1 - 9 e^{3 x},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(1 - 9 e^{3 x}\\right)-\\left(3 e^{3 x}\\right)=\\left(1\\right)+\\left(- 9 e^{3 x}\\right)-\\left(3 e^{3 x}\\right)=1 - 12 e^{3 x}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=9 e^{3 x + 3 y} + \\frac{1}{8}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=9 e^{3 x + 3 y} + \\frac{9}{8},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(9 e^{3 x + 3 y} + \\frac{9}{8}\\right)-\\left(9 e^{3 x + 3 y} + \\frac{1}{8}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{9}{8}\\right)+\\left(9 e^{3 x + 3 y}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)-\\left(9 e^{3 x + 3 y}\\right)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "According to Green's theorem, if $F(x,y)=⟨P(x,y),\\,Q(x,y)⟩,$ where\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Vector field I gives the required area. In this case,\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=9 y^{2} - \\frac{1}{8}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=9 y^{2} + \\frac{7}{8},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(9 y^{2} + \\frac{7}{8}\\right)-\\left(9 y^{2} - \\frac{1}{8}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)+\\left(9 y^{2}\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{1}{8}\\right)-\\left(9 y^{2}\\right)=1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II does *not* give the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3 e^{3 x}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1 - 9 e^{3 x},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(1 - 9 e^{3 x}\\right)-\\left(3 e^{3 x}\\right)=\\left(1\\right)+\\left(- 9 e^{3 x}\\right)-\\left(3 e^{3 x}\\right)=1 - 12 e^{3 x}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III gives the required area. Notice that\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{∂P}{∂y}=9 e^{3 x + 3 y} + \\frac{1}{8}\n$$\nand\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=9 e^{3 x + 3 y} + \\frac{9}{8},\n$$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(9 e^{3 x + 3 y} + \\frac{9}{8}\\right)-\\left(9 e^{3 x + 3 y} + \\frac{1}{8}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{9}{8}\\right)+\\left(9 e^{3 x + 3 y}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)-\\left(9 e^{3 x + 3 y}\\right)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186561533 :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the vector field $F(x,y)?$\n\nI. $F(x,y)=⟨3 y^{3} - \\frac{y}{8},\\,9 x y^{2} + \\frac{7 x}{8}⟩$\n\nII. $F(x,y)=⟨3 y e^{2 x},\\,x - 3 e^{2 x}⟩$\n\nIII. $F(x,y)=⟨\\frac{y}{8} + 2 e^{2 x + 2 y},\\,\\frac{9 x}{8} + 2 e^{2 x + 2 y}⟩$" "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the vector field $F(x,y)?$\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle F(x,y)=⟨3 y^{3} - \\frac{y}{8},\\,9 x y^{2} + \\frac{7 x}{8}⟩\n$$\n\nII. $F(x,y)=⟨3 y e^{2 x},\\,x - 3 e^{2 x}⟩$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle F(x,y)=⟨\\frac{y}{8} + 2 e^{2 x + 2 y},\\,\\frac{9 x}{8} + 2 e^{2 x + 2 y}⟩\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186561533 :question/worked-solution "According to Green's theorem, if $F(x,y)=⟨P(x,y),\\,Q(x,y)⟩,$ where\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Vector field I gives the required area. In this case,\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=9 y^{2} - \\frac{1}{8}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=9 y^{2} + \\frac{7}{8},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(9 y^{2} + \\frac{7}{8}\\right)-\\left(9 y^{2} - \\frac{1}{8}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)+\\left(9 y^{2}\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{1}{8}\\right)-\\left(9 y^{2}\\right)=1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II does *not* give the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3 e^{2 x}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1 - 6 e^{2 x},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(1 - 6 e^{2 x}\\right)-\\left(3 e^{2 x}\\right)=\\left(1\\right)+\\left(- 6 e^{2 x}\\right)-\\left(3 e^{2 x}\\right)=1 - 9 e^{2 x}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=4 e^{2 x + 2 y} + \\frac{1}{8}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4 e^{2 x + 2 y} + \\frac{9}{8},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(4 e^{2 x + 2 y} + \\frac{9}{8}\\right)-\\left(4 e^{2 x + 2 y} + \\frac{1}{8}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{9}{8}\\right)+\\left(4 e^{2 x + 2 y}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)-\\left(4 e^{2 x + 2 y}\\right)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "According to Green's theorem, if $F(x,y)=⟨P(x,y),\\,Q(x,y)⟩,$ where\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Vector field I gives the required area. In this case,\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=9 y^{2} - \\frac{1}{8}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=9 y^{2} + \\frac{7}{8},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(9 y^{2} + \\frac{7}{8}\\right)-\\left(9 y^{2} - \\frac{1}{8}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)+\\left(9 y^{2}\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{1}{8}\\right)-\\left(9 y^{2}\\right)=1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II does *not* give the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3 e^{2 x}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1 - 6 e^{2 x},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(1 - 6 e^{2 x}\\right)-\\left(3 e^{2 x}\\right)=\\left(1\\right)+\\left(- 6 e^{2 x}\\right)-\\left(3 e^{2 x}\\right)=1 - 9 e^{2 x}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III gives the required area. Notice that\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{∂P}{∂y}=4 e^{2 x + 2 y} + \\frac{1}{8}\n$$\nand\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4 e^{2 x + 2 y} + \\frac{9}{8},\n$$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(4 e^{2 x + 2 y} + \\frac{9}{8}\\right)-\\left(4 e^{2 x + 2 y} + \\frac{1}{8}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{9}{8}\\right)+\\left(4 e^{2 x + 2 y}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)-\\left(4 e^{2 x + 2 y}\\right)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186561538 :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the vector field $F(x,y)?$\n\nI. $F(x,y)=⟨\\frac{y}{6} + 3 e^{3 x + 2 y},\\,\\frac{7 x}{6} + 2 e^{3 x + 2 y}⟩$\n\nII. $F(x,y)=⟨y^{2} - \\frac{y}{6},\\,2 x y + \\frac{5 x}{6}⟩$\n\nIII. $F(x,y)=⟨y e^{3 x},\\,x - e^{3 x}⟩$" "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the vector field $F(x,y)?$\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle F(x,y)=⟨\\frac{y}{6} + 3 e^{3 x + 2 y},\\,\\frac{7 x}{6} + 2 e^{3 x + 2 y}⟩\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle F(x,y)=⟨y^{2} - \\frac{y}{6},\\,2 x y + \\frac{5 x}{6}⟩\n$$\n\nIII. $F(x,y)=⟨y e^{3 x},\\,x - e^{3 x}⟩$"] [:db/cas 17592186561538 :question/worked-solution "According to Green's theorem, if $F(x,y)=⟨P(x,y),\\,Q(x,y)⟩,$ where\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Vector field I gives the required area. In this case,\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=6 e^{3 x + 2 y} + \\frac{1}{6}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=6 e^{3 x + 2 y} + \\frac{7}{6},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(6 e^{3 x + 2 y} + \\frac{7}{6}\\right)-\\left(6 e^{3 x + 2 y} + \\frac{1}{6}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{7}{6}\\right)+\\left(6 e^{3 x + 2 y}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)-\\left(6 e^{3 x + 2 y}\\right)=1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2 y - \\frac{1}{6}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2 y + \\frac{5}{6},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(2 y + \\frac{5}{6}\\right)-\\left(2 y - \\frac{1}{6}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)+\\left(2 y\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{1}{6}\\right)-\\left(2 y\\right)=1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III does *not* give the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=e^{3 x}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1 - 3 e^{3 x},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(1 - 3 e^{3 x}\\right)-\\left(e^{3 x}\\right)=\\left(1\\right)+\\left(- 3 e^{3 x}\\right)-\\left(e^{3 x}\\right)=1 - 4 e^{3 x}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "According to Green's theorem, if $F(x,y)=⟨P(x,y),\\,Q(x,y)⟩,$ where\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Vector field I gives the required area. In this case,\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{∂P}{∂y}=6 e^{3 x + 2 y} + \\frac{1}{6}\n$$\nand\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=6 e^{3 x + 2 y} + \\frac{7}{6},\n$$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(6 e^{3 x + 2 y} + \\frac{7}{6}\\right)-\\left(6 e^{3 x + 2 y} + \\frac{1}{6}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{7}{6}\\right)+\\left(6 e^{3 x + 2 y}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)-\\left(6 e^{3 x + 2 y}\\right)=1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2 y - \\frac{1}{6}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2 y + \\frac{5}{6},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(2 y + \\frac{5}{6}\\right)-\\left(2 y - \\frac{1}{6}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)+\\left(2 y\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{1}{6}\\right)-\\left(2 y\\right)=1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III does *not* give the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=e^{3 x}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1 - 3 e^{3 x},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(1 - 3 e^{3 x}\\right)-\\left(e^{3 x}\\right)=\\left(1\\right)+\\left(- 3 e^{3 x}\\right)-\\left(e^{3 x}\\right)=1 - 4 e^{3 x}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186561545 :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the vector field $F(x,y)?$\n\nI. $F(x,y)=⟨\\frac{y}{8} + 3 e^{3 x + y},\\,\\frac{9 x}{8} + e^{3 x + y}⟩$\n\nII. $F(x,y)=⟨2 y^{3} - \\frac{y}{8},\\,6 x y^{2} + \\frac{7 x}{8}⟩$\n\nIII. $F(x,y)=⟨2 y e^{3 x},\\,x - 2 e^{3 x}⟩$" "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the vector field $F(x,y)?$\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle F(x,y)=⟨\\frac{y}{8} + 3 e^{3 x + y},\\,\\frac{9 x}{8} + e^{3 x + y}⟩\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle F(x,y)=⟨2 y^{3} - \\frac{y}{8},\\,6 x y^{2} + \\frac{7 x}{8}⟩\n$$\n\nIII. $F(x,y)=⟨2 y e^{3 x},\\,x - 2 e^{3 x}⟩$"] [:db/cas 17592186561545 :question/worked-solution "According to Green's theorem, if $F(x,y)=⟨P(x,y),\\,Q(x,y)⟩,$ where\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Vector field I gives the required area. In this case,\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3 e^{3 x + y} + \\frac{1}{8}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3 e^{3 x + y} + \\frac{9}{8},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(3 e^{3 x + y} + \\frac{9}{8}\\right)-\\left(3 e^{3 x + y} + \\frac{1}{8}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{9}{8}\\right)+\\left(3 e^{3 x + y}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)-\\left(3 e^{3 x + y}\\right)=1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=6 y^{2} - \\frac{1}{8}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=6 y^{2} + \\frac{7}{8},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(6 y^{2} + \\frac{7}{8}\\right)-\\left(6 y^{2} - \\frac{1}{8}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)+\\left(6 y^{2}\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{1}{8}\\right)-\\left(6 y^{2}\\right)=1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III does *not* give the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2 e^{3 x}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1 - 6 e^{3 x},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(1 - 6 e^{3 x}\\right)-\\left(2 e^{3 x}\\right)=\\left(1\\right)+\\left(- 6 e^{3 x}\\right)-\\left(2 e^{3 x}\\right)=1 - 8 e^{3 x}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "According to Green's theorem, if $F(x,y)=⟨P(x,y),\\,Q(x,y)⟩,$ where\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Vector field I gives the required area. In this case,\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3 e^{3 x + y} + \\frac{1}{8}\n$$\nand\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3 e^{3 x + y} + \\frac{9}{8},\n$$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(3 e^{3 x + y} + \\frac{9}{8}\\right)-\\left(3 e^{3 x + y} + \\frac{1}{8}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{9}{8}\\right)+\\left(3 e^{3 x + y}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)-\\left(3 e^{3 x + y}\\right)=1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=6 y^{2} - \\frac{1}{8}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=6 y^{2} + \\frac{7}{8},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(6 y^{2} + \\frac{7}{8}\\right)-\\left(6 y^{2} - \\frac{1}{8}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)+\\left(6 y^{2}\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{1}{8}\\right)-\\left(6 y^{2}\\right)=1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III does *not* give the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2 e^{3 x}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1 - 6 e^{3 x},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(1 - 6 e^{3 x}\\right)-\\left(2 e^{3 x}\\right)=\\left(1\\right)+\\left(- 6 e^{3 x}\\right)-\\left(2 e^{3 x}\\right)=1 - 8 e^{3 x}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186561555 :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the vector field $F(x,y)?$\n\nI. $F(x,y)=⟨3 y^{2} - \\frac{y}{4},\\,6 x y + \\frac{3 x}{4}⟩$\n\nII. $F(x,y)=⟨\\frac{y}{4} + 3 e^{3 x + 3 y},\\,\\frac{5 x}{4} + 3 e^{3 x + 3 y}⟩$\n\nIII. $F(x,y)=⟨3 y e^{3 x},\\,x - 3 e^{3 x}⟩$" "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the vector field $F(x,y)?$\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle F(x,y)=⟨3 y^{2} - \\frac{y}{4},\\,6 x y + \\frac{3 x}{4}⟩\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle F(x,y)=⟨\\frac{y}{4} + 3 e^{3 x + 3 y},\\,\\frac{5 x}{4} + 3 e^{3 x + 3 y}⟩\n$$\n\nIII. $F(x,y)=⟨3 y e^{3 x},\\,x - 3 e^{3 x}⟩$"] [:db/cas 17592186561555 :question/worked-solution "According to Green's theorem, if $F(x,y)=⟨P(x,y),\\,Q(x,y)⟩,$ where\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Vector field I gives the required area. In this case,\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=6 y - \\frac{1}{4}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=6 y + \\frac{3}{4},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(6 y + \\frac{3}{4}\\right)-\\left(6 y - \\frac{1}{4}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)+\\left(6 y\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{1}{4}\\right)-\\left(6 y\\right)=1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=9 e^{3 x + 3 y} + \\frac{1}{4}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=9 e^{3 x + 3 y} + \\frac{5}{4},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(9 e^{3 x + 3 y} + \\frac{5}{4}\\right)-\\left(9 e^{3 x + 3 y} + \\frac{1}{4}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)+\\left(9 e^{3 x + 3 y}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)-\\left(9 e^{3 x + 3 y}\\right)=1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III does *not* give the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3 e^{3 x}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1 - 9 e^{3 x},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(1 - 9 e^{3 x}\\right)-\\left(3 e^{3 x}\\right)=\\left(1\\right)+\\left(- 9 e^{3 x}\\right)-\\left(3 e^{3 x}\\right)=1 - 12 e^{3 x}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "According to Green's theorem, if $F(x,y)=⟨P(x,y),\\,Q(x,y)⟩,$ where\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Vector field I gives the required area. In this case,\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=6 y - \\frac{1}{4}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=6 y + \\frac{3}{4},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(6 y + \\frac{3}{4}\\right)-\\left(6 y - \\frac{1}{4}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)+\\left(6 y\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{1}{4}\\right)-\\left(6 y\\right)=1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II gives the required area. Notice that\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{∂P}{∂y}=9 e^{3 x + 3 y} + \\frac{1}{4}\n$$\nand\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=9 e^{3 x + 3 y} + \\frac{5}{4},\n$$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(9 e^{3 x + 3 y} + \\frac{5}{4}\\right)-\\left(9 e^{3 x + 3 y} + \\frac{1}{4}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)+\\left(9 e^{3 x + 3 y}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)-\\left(9 e^{3 x + 3 y}\\right)=1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III does *not* give the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3 e^{3 x}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1 - 9 e^{3 x},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(1 - 9 e^{3 x}\\right)-\\left(3 e^{3 x}\\right)=\\left(1\\right)+\\left(- 9 e^{3 x}\\right)-\\left(3 e^{3 x}\\right)=1 - 12 e^{3 x}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186562465 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\n- From the matrix $C=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}],$ we deduce that $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=-1$ and $\\text{Im}(\\lambda )=3.$ So, our eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-1+3\\text{i}=-1+3\\text{i}.\n$$\n\n- From the matrix $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}],$ we deduce that our corresponding eigenvector is\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1+3\\text{i} \\\\ -1+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1+3\\text{i} \\\\ -1+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda ,$ and the second eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nTherefore, our eigenvalues and eigenvectors are \n\n$$\n\\lambda =-1\\pm 3\\text{i},\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1\\pm 3\\text{i} \\\\ -1\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\n- From the matrix $$\n\\displaystyle C=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we deduce that $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=-1$ and $\\text{Im}(\\lambda )=3.$ So, our eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-1+3\\text{i}=-1+3\\text{i}.\n$$\n\n- From the matrix $$\n\\displaystyle P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we deduce that our corresponding eigenvector is\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1+3\\text{i} \\\\ -1+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1+3\\text{i} \\\\ -1+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda ,$ and the second eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nTherefore, our eigenvalues and eigenvectors are \n\n$$\n\\lambda =-1\\pm 3\\text{i},\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1\\pm 3\\text{i} \\\\ -1\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186562502 :question/problem "Which of the following vector spaces are finite-dimensional?\n\nI. ${\\text{M}}_{200\\times 800}(R),$ the vector space of all $200\\times 800$ matrices over $R$\n\nII. ${R}_{500}[t],$ the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $500$\n\nIII. ${L}^{1}([-1,1]),$ the vector space of all functions  $f:[-1,1]\\to R$ such that ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}|f(x)|\\,\\text{d}x<\\infty $" "Which of the following vector spaces are finite-dimensional?\n\nI. ${\\text{M}}_{200\\times 800}(R),$ the vector space of all $200\\times 800$ matrices over $R$\n\nII. ${R}_{500}[t],$ the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $500$\n\nIII. ${L}^{1}([-1,1]),$ the vector space of all functions  $f:[-1,1]\\to R$ such that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1}^{1}|f(x)|\\,\\text{d}x<\\infty \n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186562503 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=2x(1-x)-xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-2y+4xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x(1-x)-xy)=2-4x-y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x(1-x)-xy)=-x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2y+4xy)=4y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2y+4xy)=-2+4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}2-4x-y & -x \\\\ 4y & -2+4x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0.5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}2-4(0.5)-(1) & -(0.5) \\\\ 4(1) & -2+4(0.5)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -0.5 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & -0.5 \\\\ 4 & -\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )-4\\cdot (-0.5) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda +2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=-0.5\\pm 0.5\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i}.$ Since the eigenvalues are complex with negative real parts, our equilibrium is $\\text{an asymptotically stable}$ $\\text{spiral sink}.$" "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=2x(1-x)-xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-2y+4xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x(1-x)-xy)=2-4x-y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x(1-x)-xy)=-x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2y+4xy)=4y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2y+4xy)=-2+4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}2-4x-y & -x \\\\ 4y & -2+4x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium $$\n\\displaystyle {x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0.5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}2-4(0.5)-(1) & -(0.5) \\\\ 4(1) & -2+4(0.5)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -0.5 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & -0.5 \\\\ 4 & -\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )-4\\cdot (-0.5) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda +2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=-0.5\\pm 0.5\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i}.$ Since the eigenvalues are complex with negative real parts, our equilibrium is $\\text{an asymptotically stable}$ $\\text{spiral sink}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186562511 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\n- From the matrix $C=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -(-2) & 4\\end{aligned}],$ we deduce that $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=4$ and $\\text{Im}(\\lambda )=-2.$ So, our eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\lambda =4+(-2)\\text{i}=4-2\\text{i}.\n$$\n\n- From the matrix $P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}],$ we deduce that our corresponding eigenvector is\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}2+3\\text{i} \\\\ -4+(-1)\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2+3\\text{i} \\\\ -4-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda ,$ and the second eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nTherefore, our eigenvalues and eigenvectors are \n\n$$\n\\lambda =4∓2\\text{i},\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}2\\pm 3\\text{i} \\\\ -4∓\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\n- From the matrix $$\n\\displaystyle C=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -(-2) & 4\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we deduce that $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=4$ and $\\text{Im}(\\lambda )=-2.$ So, our eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\lambda =4+(-2)\\text{i}=4-2\\text{i}.\n$$\n\n- From the matrix $$\n\\displaystyle P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we deduce that our corresponding eigenvector is\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}2+3\\text{i} \\\\ -4+(-1)\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2+3\\text{i} \\\\ -4-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda ,$ and the second eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nTherefore, our eigenvalues and eigenvectors are \n\n$$\n\\lambda =4∓2\\text{i},\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}2\\pm 3\\text{i} \\\\ -4∓\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186562768 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $\\oint_C(e^{-x^2}-4x^2y)\\,dx+(y+4xy^2)\\,dy,$ where $C$ is the counterclockwise boundary of the disk with polar description $0\\le r\\le 2,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi$." "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C(e^{-x^2}-4x^2y)\\,dx+(y+4xy^2)\\,dy,\n$$ where $C$ is the counterclockwise boundary of the disk with polar description $0\\le r\\le 2,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi$."] [:db/cas 17592186562773 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $\\oint_C(e^{-x^2}-x^2y)\\,dx+(y+xy^2)\\,dy,$ where $C$ is the counterclockwise boundary of the disk with polar description $0\\le r\\le 1,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi$." "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C(e^{-x^2}-x^2y)\\,dx+(y+xy^2)\\,dy,\n$$ where $C$ is the counterclockwise boundary of the disk with polar description $0\\le r\\le 1,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi$."] [:db/cas 17592186562783 :question/problem "![](images/5d/5def443330d0f3793a305101ad63ea19d8a6e0a7835a23018063bd6fb57d23a8.png)\n\nEvaluate $\\oint_C(\\sqrt{5+x}-5x^2y)\\,dx+(e^{-y^2}+5xy^2)\\,dy,$ where the path $C$ is shown above." "![](images/5d/5def443330d0f3793a305101ad63ea19d8a6e0a7835a23018063bd6fb57d23a8.png)\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C(\\sqrt{5+x}-5x^2y)\\,dx+(e^{-y^2}+5xy^2)\\,dy,\n$$ where the path $C$ is shown above."] [:db/cas 17592186562793 :question/problem "![](images/9f/9f98c6d615262205474643c73e3d4b5bd8d94a8560d0ffbda34ad7aca362d1bd.png)\n\nEvaluate $\\oint_C(\\sqrt{x+1}-x^2y)\\,dx+(e^y+xy^2)\\,dy,$ where the path $C$ is shown above." "![](images/9f/9f98c6d615262205474643c73e3d4b5bd8d94a8560d0ffbda34ad7aca362d1bd.png)\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C(\\sqrt{x+1}-x^2y)\\,dx+(e^y+xy^2)\\,dy,\n$$ where the path $C$ is shown above."] [:db/cas 17592186562801 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $\\oint_C(e^{-x^2}-x^2y)\\,dx+(y+xy^2)\\,dy,$ where $C$ is the counterclockwise boundary of the disk with polar description $0\\le r\\le 3,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi$." "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C(e^{-x^2}-x^2y)\\,dx+(y+xy^2)\\,dy,\n$$ where $C$ is the counterclockwise boundary of the disk with polar description $0\\le r\\le 3,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi$."] [:db/cas 17592186562805 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $\\oint_C(e^{-x^2}-3x^2y)\\,dx+(y+3xy^2)\\,dy,$ where $C$ is the counterclockwise boundary of the disk with polar description $0\\le r\\le 3,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi$." "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C(e^{-x^2}-3x^2y)\\,dx+(y+3xy^2)\\,dy,\n$$ where $C$ is the counterclockwise boundary of the disk with polar description $0\\le r\\le 3,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi$."] [:db/cas 17592186562808 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $\\oint_C(e^{-x^2}-3x^2y)\\,dx+(y+3xy^2)\\,dy,$ where $C$ is the counterclockwise boundary of the disk with polar description $0\\le r\\le 4,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi$." "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C(e^{-x^2}-3x^2y)\\,dx+(y+3xy^2)\\,dy,\n$$ where $C$ is the counterclockwise boundary of the disk with polar description $0\\le r\\le 4,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi$."] [:db/cas 17592186563648 :question/problem "Determine the interval on which the function $f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-1} \\\\ \\sqrt{t} \\\\ t+3\\end{aligned}]$  is continuous." "Determine the interval on which the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-1} \\\\ \\sqrt{t} \\\\ t+3\\end{aligned}]\n$$  is continuous."] [:db/cas 17592186563651 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{16-(3x+2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{16-(3x+2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563662 :question/problem "Find the limit ${lim}_{t\\to 1}‖f(t)‖$, if $f(t)=\\frac{4+t}{5}\\,i+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-1)}{{t}^{2}-1}\\,j+\\sqrt{9t}\\,k.$" "Find the limit ${lim}_{t\\to 1}‖f(t)‖$, if $$\n\\displaystyle f(t)=\\frac{4+t}{5}\\,i+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-1)}{{t}^{2}-1}\\,j+\\sqrt{9t}\\,k.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563667 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{s\\to 0}f(s)$, if $f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{s+8} \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡s}{s} \\\\ (1+2s)^{1/(2s)}\\end{aligned}].$" "Calculate ${lim}_{s\\to 0}f(s)$, if $$\n\\displaystyle f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{s+8} \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡s}{s} \\\\ (1+2s)^{1/(2s)}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563667 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{s\\to 0}f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}\\sqrt{s+8} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡s}{s} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 0}(1+2s)^{1/(2s)}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}\\sqrt{s+8} & =\\sqrt{0+8} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{3}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second component can be found using L'Hopital's Rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡s}{s} & ={lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{{(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡s)}^{′}}{{s}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{s}^{2}}}}{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{s}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third component can be found using a special limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}(1+2s)^{1/(2s)} & ={lim}_{u\\to 0}(1+u)^{1/u}=e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{s\\to 0}f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ e\\end{aligned}].$" "First, recall that:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{s\\to 0}f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}\\sqrt{s+8} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡s}{s} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 0}(1+2s)^{1/(2s)}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}\\sqrt{s+8} & =\\sqrt{0+8} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{3}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second component can be found using L'Hopital's Rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡s}{s} & ={lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{{(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡s)}^{′}}{{s}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{s}^{2}}}}{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{s}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third component can be found using a special limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}(1+2s)^{1/(2s)} & ={lim}_{u\\to 0}(1+u)^{1/u}=e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{s\\to 0}f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ e\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563668 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{|x-5|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-10x+27}}\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{|x-5|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-10x+27}}\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563693 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{s\\to 1}‖f(s)‖,$ if $f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5-s} \\\\ \\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{s} \\\\ (1+5s)^{1-s}\\end{aligned}].$" "Calculate ${lim}_{s\\to 1}‖f(s)‖,$ if $$\n\\displaystyle f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5-s} \\\\ \\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{s} \\\\ (1+5s)^{1-s}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563693 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{s\\to 1}f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\sqrt{5-s} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{1-s} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 1}\\,(1+5s)^{1/(2s)}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one by one.\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\sqrt{5-s} & =\\sqrt{5-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{s}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\,(1+5s)^{1-s} & =(1+5\\cdot 1)^{1-1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{s\\to 1}f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, the limit of $‖f(s)‖$ as $s$ approaches $1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}‖f(s)‖ & =‖{lim}_{s\\to 1}f(s)‖ \\\\  & =‖[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, recall that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{s\\to 1}f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\sqrt{5-s} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{1-s} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 1}\\,(1+5s)^{1/(2s)}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one by one.\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\sqrt{5-s} & =\\sqrt{5-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{s}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\,(1+5s)^{1-s} & =(1+5\\cdot 1)^{1-1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{s\\to 1}f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the limit of $‖f(s)‖$ as $s$ approaches $1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}‖f(s)‖ & =‖{lim}_{s\\to 1}f(s)‖ \\\\  & =‖[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563699 :question/problem "$\\int -\\frac{15}{|5x+10|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4x+5}}\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int -\\frac{15}{|5x+10|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4x+5}}\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563702 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(1) \\\\ y(1)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"130px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(1) \\\\ y(1)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}],\n$$ where $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"130px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [:db/cas 17592186563702 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nSo, we can write our general solution as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system. Substituting $t={t}_{0},$ we have\n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c\n$$\n\nand since ${e}^{At}$ is invertible for all $t,$ we have\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nPlugging the above back into our expression for $x(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{A(t-1)}\\,x(1) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-1)) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-1)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-1)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-1))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-1))\\, \\\\ \\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-1))\\,\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$ So, the matrix of the system is $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nSo, we can write our general solution as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system. Substituting $t={t}_{0},$ we have\n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c\n$$\n\nand since ${e}^{At}$ is invertible for all $t,$ we have\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nPlugging the above back into our expression for $x(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{A(t-1)}\\,x(1) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-1)) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-1)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-1)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-1))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-1))\\, \\\\ \\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-1))\\,\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563704 :question/problem "Consider the two sets of $8$ paired observations in the table below.\n\n| **$\\,{x}_{i}\\,$** | $16$ | $9$ | $12$ | $10$ | $11$ | $11$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **$\\,{y}_{i}\\,$** | $10$ | $14$ | $8$ | $10$ | $7$ | $8$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n\nGiven that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{x}_{i}{y}_{i}=695,$ calculate ${S}_{xy}.$" "Consider the two sets of $8$ paired observations in the table below.\n\n| **$\\,{x}_{i}\\,$** | $16$ | $9$ | $12$ | $10$ | $11$ | $11$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **$\\,{y}_{i}\\,$** | $10$ | $14$ | $8$ | $10$ | $7$ | $8$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n\nGiven that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{x}_{i}{y}_{i}=695,\n$$ calculate ${S}_{xy}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186563704 :question/worked-solution "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ the sum of products of deviations from the means can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}{y}_{i}-\\frac{1}{n}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the sum of the ${x}_{i}$ and ${y}_{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =16+9+12+10+11+11+6+9=84, \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i} & =10+14+8+10+7+8+2+4=63\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{x}_{i}{y}_{i}=695.$\n\nFinally, by substituting in the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n{S}_{xy}=695-\\frac{1}{8}(84)(63)=695-661.5=33.5.\n$$" "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ the sum of products of deviations from the means can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}{y}_{i}-\\frac{1}{n}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the sum of the ${x}_{i}$ and ${y}_{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =16+9+12+10+11+11+6+9=84, \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i} & =10+14+8+10+7+8+2+4=63\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{x}_{i}{y}_{i}=695.\n$$\n\nFinally, by substituting in the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n{S}_{xy}=695-\\frac{1}{8}(84)(63)=695-661.5=33.5.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563711 :question/problem "Determine the interval on which the function $f(t)=\\frac{{t}^{2}+4t}{t}\\,i+({t}^{2}-1)\\,j+\\frac{1}{t-2}\\,k$ is continuous." "Determine the interval on which the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(t)=\\frac{{t}^{2}+4t}{t}\\,i+({t}^{2}-1)\\,j+\\frac{1}{t-2}\\,k\n$$ is continuous."] [:db/cas 17592186563712 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{t\\to 3}‖f(t)‖$, if $f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}-3t \\\\ \\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-8} \\\\ 5-2t\\end{aligned}].$" "Calculate ${lim}_{t\\to 3}‖f(t)‖$, if $$\n\\displaystyle f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}-3t \\\\ \\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-8} \\\\ 5-2t\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563712 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 3}f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 3}({t}^{2}-3t) \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 3}\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-8} \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 3}(5-2t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 3}({t}^{2}-3t) & ={3}^{2}-3(3)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 3}\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-8} & =\\frac{1}{{3}^{2}-8}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 3}(5-2t) & =5-2(3)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{t\\to 3}f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, the limit of $‖f(t)‖$ as $t$ approaches $3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 3}‖f(t)‖ & =‖{lim}_{t\\to 3}f(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, recall that:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 3}f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 3}({t}^{2}-3t) \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 3}\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-8} \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 3}(5-2t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 3}({t}^{2}-3t) & ={3}^{2}-3(3)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 3}\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-8} & =\\frac{1}{{3}^{2}-8}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 3}(5-2t) & =5-2(3)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{t\\to 3}f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the limit of $‖f(t)‖$ as $t$ approaches $3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 3}‖f(t)‖ & =‖{lim}_{t\\to 3}f(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563716 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{s\\to 0}‖f(s)‖$, if $f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{s}{(s+1)^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡s}{{s}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s}{2{s}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\,.$" "Calculate ${lim}_{s\\to 0}‖f(s)‖$, if $$\n\\displaystyle f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{s}{(s+1)^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡s}{{s}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s}{2{s}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\,.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563716 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}f(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{s}{(s+1)^{2}} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡s}{{s}^{2}} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s}{2{s}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{s}{(s+1)^{2}} & =\\frac{0}{(0+1)^{2}}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second component can be found using a special limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡s}{{s}^{2}} & ={lim}_{s\\to 0}{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡s}{s}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ={({lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡s}{s})}^{2} \\\\  & ={1}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third component can be found using L'Hopital's Rule twice:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s}{2{s}^{2}} & ={lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s)}^{″}}{{(2{s}^{2})}^{″}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡s}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{s\\to 0}f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1/4\\end{aligned}]\\,.$\n\nFinally, the limit of $‖f(s)‖$ as $s$ approaches $0$ is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}‖f(s)‖ & =‖{lim}_{s\\to 0}f(s)‖ \\\\  & =‖[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1/4\\end{aligned}]‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{17}{16}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, recall that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}f(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{s}{(s+1)^{2}} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡s}{{s}^{2}} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s}{2{s}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{s}{(s+1)^{2}} & =\\frac{0}{(0+1)^{2}}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second component can be found using a special limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡s}{{s}^{2}} & ={lim}_{s\\to 0}{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡s}{s}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ={({lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡s}{s})}^{2} \\\\  & ={1}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third component can be found using L'Hopital's Rule twice:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s}{2{s}^{2}} & ={lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s)}^{″}}{{(2{s}^{2})}^{″}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡s}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{s\\to 0}f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1/4\\end{aligned}]\\,.\n$$\n\nFinally, the limit of $‖f(s)‖$ as $s$ approaches $0$ is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}‖f(s)‖ & =‖{lim}_{s\\to 0}f(s)‖ \\\\  & =‖[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1/4\\end{aligned}]‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{17}{16}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563720 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{(x+2)\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-4x}}\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{(x+2)\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-4x}}\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563744 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{3}{(x+3)\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-6x}}\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{3}{(x+3)\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-6x}}\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563754 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{s\\to 1}f(s),$ if $f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5-s} \\\\ \\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{s} \\\\ (1+5s)^{1-s}\\end{aligned}].$" "Calculate ${lim}_{s\\to 1}f(s),$ if $$\n\\displaystyle f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5-s} \\\\ \\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{s} \\\\ (1+5s)^{1-s}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563754 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{s\\to 1}f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\sqrt{5-s} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{s} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 1}\\,(1+5s)^{1/(2s)}.\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one by one.\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\sqrt{5-s} & =\\sqrt{5-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{s}=\\frac{{e}^{1-1}}{1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\,(1+5s)^{1-s} & =(1+5\\cdot 1)^{1-1} \\\\  & ={6}^{0} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{s\\to 1}f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" "First, recall that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{s\\to 1}f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\sqrt{5-s} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{s} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 1}\\,(1+5s)^{1/(2s)}.\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one by one.\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\sqrt{5-s} & =\\sqrt{5-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{s}=\\frac{{e}^{1-1}}{1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\,(1+5s)^{1-s} & =(1+5\\cdot 1)^{1-1} \\\\  & ={6}^{0} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{s\\to 1}f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563755 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives the general solution to the system of ODEs above?\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t) & -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*" "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives the general solution to the system of ODEs above?\n\n*Hint: $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t) & -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}],\n$$ where $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$*"] [:db/cas 17592186563755 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t) & -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c \\\\  & ={e}^{At}\\,c \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t) & -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.$" "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$ So, the matrix of the system is $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t) & -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c \\\\  & ={e}^{At}\\,c \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t) & -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.$"] [:db/cas 17592186563824 :question/problem "Given that ${∮}_C F\\cdot dr=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^{6}\\boxed{}\\,dr\\,d\\theta$, where $F(x,y)=⟨3{y}^{3}+{e}^{x},\\,\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-6{x}^{3}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle $x^2+y^2=36$ oriented counterclockwise, what expression belongs in the blank after applying Green’s theorem and changing to polar coordinates, simplified pointwise before integration?" "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_C F\\cdot dr=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^{6}\\boxed{}\\,dr\\,d\\theta,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=⟨3{y}^{3}+{e}^{x},\\,\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-6{x}^{3}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle $x^2+y^2=36$ oriented counterclockwise, what expression belongs in the blank after applying Green’s theorem and changing to polar coordinates, simplified pointwise before integration?"] [:db/cas 17592186563828 :question/problem "Given that ${∮}_C F\\cdot dr=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^{5}\\boxed{}\\,dr\\,d\\theta$, where $F(x,y)=⟨3{y}^{3}+{e}^{x},\\,\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-2{x}^{3}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle $x^2+y^2=25$ oriented counterclockwise, what expression belongs in the blank after applying Green’s theorem and changing to polar coordinates, simplified pointwise before integration?" "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_C F\\cdot dr=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^{5}\\boxed{}\\,dr\\,d\\theta,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=⟨3{y}^{3}+{e}^{x},\\,\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-2{x}^{3}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle $x^2+y^2=25$ oriented counterclockwise, what expression belongs in the blank after applying Green’s theorem and changing to polar coordinates, simplified pointwise before integration?"] [:db/cas 17592186563837 :question/problem "Given that ${∮}_C F\\cdot dr=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^{1}\\boxed{}\\,dr\\,d\\theta$, where $F(x,y)=⟨6{y}^{3}+{e}^{x},\\,\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-3{x}^{3}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle $x^2+y^2=1$ oriented counterclockwise, what expression belongs in the blank after applying Green’s theorem and changing to polar coordinates, simplified pointwise before integration?" "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_C F\\cdot dr=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^{1}\\boxed{}\\,dr\\,d\\theta,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=⟨6{y}^{3}+{e}^{x},\\,\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-3{x}^{3}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle $x^2+y^2=1$ oriented counterclockwise, what expression belongs in the blank after applying Green’s theorem and changing to polar coordinates, simplified pointwise before integration?"] [:db/cas 17592186563840 :question/problem "Given that ${∮}_C F\\cdot dr=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^{2}\\boxed{}\\,dr\\,d\\theta$, where $F(x,y)=⟨4{y}^{3}+{e}^{x},\\,\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-3{x}^{3}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle $x^2+y^2=4$ oriented counterclockwise, what expression belongs in the blank after applying Green’s theorem and changing to polar coordinates, simplified pointwise before integration?" "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_C F\\cdot dr=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^{2}\\boxed{}\\,dr\\,d\\theta,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=⟨4{y}^{3}+{e}^{x},\\,\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-3{x}^{3}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle $x^2+y^2=4$ oriented counterclockwise, what expression belongs in the blank after applying Green’s theorem and changing to polar coordinates, simplified pointwise before integration?"] [:db/cas 17592186563841 :question/problem "Given that ${∮}_C F\\cdot dr=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^{5}\\boxed{}\\,dr\\,d\\theta$, where $F(x,y)=⟨6{y}^{3}+{e}^{x},\\,\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-{x}^{3}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle $x^2+y^2=25$ oriented counterclockwise, what expression belongs in the blank after applying Green’s theorem and changing to polar coordinates, simplified pointwise before integration?" "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_C F\\cdot dr=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^{5}\\boxed{}\\,dr\\,d\\theta,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=⟨6{y}^{3}+{e}^{x},\\,\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-{x}^{3}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle $x^2+y^2=25$ oriented counterclockwise, what expression belongs in the blank after applying Green’s theorem and changing to polar coordinates, simplified pointwise before integration?"] [:db/cas 17592186563860 :question/problem "Given that\n$∮_(C)F \\cdot dr = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{3}\\boxed{}drdθ$,\nwhere $F(x, y) = ⟨5xy^{2}, 5x + e^{\\cos y}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle $x^{2} + y^{2} = 9$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation? Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$, and simplify the integrand pointwise before integration." "Given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle ∮_(C)F \\cdot dr = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{3}\\boxed{}drdθ,\n$$\nwhere $F(x, y) = ⟨5xy^{2}, 5x + e^{\\cos y}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle $x^{2} + y^{2} = 9$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation? Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$, and simplify the integrand pointwise before integration."] [:db/cas 17592186563860 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as $\\oint_C F\\cdot dr=\\oint_C(5 x y^{2})\\,dx+(5 x + e^{\\cos{(y )}})\\,dy$.\n\nGreen's theorem states that $\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y})\\,dA$.\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/3b/3b1a8cf50ccf8ab3a73f31753def3c2fee2143a7372309cccf2ff7a5e68aedd9.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=5 x y^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=5 x + e^{\\cos{(y )}}$, we obtain $\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=10 x y$, $\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=5$.\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get $\\oint_C F\\cdot dr=\\iint_D(5-(10 x y))\\,dA=\\iint_D(- 10 x y + 5)\\,dA$.\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as $\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 3,0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi\\}$.\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that $\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)r\\,dr\\,d\\theta$.\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as $\\iint_D(- 10 x y + 5)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta(- 10 r^{2} \\sin{(\\theta )} \\cos{(\\theta )} + 5)r\\,dr\\,d\\theta=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^{3}r (- 5 r^{2} \\sin{(2 \\theta )} + 5)\\,dr\\,d\\theta$." "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C F\\cdot dr=\\oint_C(5 x y^{2})\\,dx+(5 x + e^{\\cos{(y )}})\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y})\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/3b/3b1a8cf50ccf8ab3a73f31753def3c2fee2143a7372309cccf2ff7a5e68aedd9.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=5 x y^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=5 x + e^{\\cos{(y )}}$, we obtain $\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=10 x y$, $\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=5$.\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C F\\cdot dr=\\iint_D(5-(10 x y))\\,dA=\\iint_D(- 10 x y + 5)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as $\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 3,0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi\\}$.\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint_D(- 10 x y + 5)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta(- 10 r^{2} \\sin{(\\theta )} \\cos{(\\theta )} + 5)r\\,dr\\,d\\theta=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^{3}r (- 5 r^{2} \\sin{(2 \\theta )} + 5)\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563918 :question/problem "Find ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ for $f(x,y)=8 y^{4} \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}+4 y \\arctan{\\left(x \\right)}- \\frac{y^{2}}{x^{2}}.$" "Find ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ for $$\n\\displaystyle f(x,y)=8 y^{4} \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}+4 y \\arctan{\\left(x \\right)}- \\frac{y^{2}}{x^{2}}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563939 :question/problem "Find ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ for $f(x,y)=8 x^{4} \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}+9 x \\arctan{\\left(y \\right)}- \\frac{3 x^{2}}{y^{3}}.$" "Find ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ for $$\n\\displaystyle f(x,y)=8 x^{4} \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}+9 x \\arctan{\\left(y \\right)}- \\frac{3 x^{2}}{y^{3}}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563954 :question/worked-solution "Remember that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion  of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$ is false, while statement ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ is true. Since the force $F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion.\n\n![](images/dc/dcc2f364085ccca85e6e6ddd8a5affac51c84f774281b817d4852bd2322bcb1b.png)\n\nSo, ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$.\n\n- Statement ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$ is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ the force is still perpendicular to the motion.\n\n![](images/50/508e0ac5f67d7b119ad689ccaede8894268eefe31d9b00f148ddd6bf851de6b4.png)\n\nSo, ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ only." "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion  of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ is false, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ is true. Since the force $F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion.\n\n![](images/dc/dcc2f364085ccca85e6e6ddd8a5affac51c84f774281b817d4852bd2322bcb1b.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ the force is still perpendicular to the motion.\n\n![](images/50/508e0ac5f67d7b119ad689ccaede8894268eefe31d9b00f148ddd6bf851de6b4.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186564480 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}-{t}^{2}+3t-2 \\\\ -4t+8\\end{aligned}],\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the missing coefficients in the solution to the initial value problem, shown above.\n\n$x(t)=$ \n{{field-1}} \n$\\,\\cdot \\,[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,-$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\,\\cdot \\,[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}-t+1 \\\\ 2t-3\\end{aligned}].$" "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}-{t}^{2}+3t-2 \\\\ -4t+8\\end{aligned}],\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the missing coefficients in the solution to the initial value problem, shown above.\n\n$x(t)=$ \n{{field-1}} \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\cdot \\,[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,-\n$$\n{{field-2}}\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\cdot \\,[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}-t+1 \\\\ 2t-3\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186564481 :question/problem "The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]?$" "The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n$$\n\\displaystyle {a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186564485 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ 1-x\\end{aligned}],\\,x\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of the zero-vector do not satisfy the equation $x+y=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & =1 \\\\ (0)+(0) & =1 \\\\ 0 & =1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ from $L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0+1 \\\\ 1+0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHowever, $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\notin L$ since substituting the coordinates into the equation, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & =1 \\\\ (1)+(1) & =1 \\\\ 2 & =1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ 1-x\\end{aligned}],\\,x\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of the zero-vector do not satisfy the equation $x+y=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & =1 \\\\ (0)+(0) & =1 \\\\ 0 & =1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let, for example, $$\n\\displaystyle a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$ from $L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0+1 \\\\ 1+0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHowever, $$\n\\displaystyle [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\notin L\n$$ since substituting the coordinates into the equation, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & =1 \\\\ (1)+(1) & =1 \\\\ 2 & =1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186564493 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ -3x+4\\end{aligned}],\\,x\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of the zero-vector do not satisfy the equation $y=-3x+4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x+4 \\\\ (0) & =-3(0)+4 \\\\ 0 & =4\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let, for example,$a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ from $L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0+1 \\\\ 4+1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHowever, $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\notin L$ since substituting the coordinates into the equation, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x+4 \\\\ (5) & =-3(1)+4 \\\\ 5 & =1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ -3x+4\\end{aligned}],\\,x\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of the zero-vector do not satisfy the equation $y=-3x+4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x+4 \\\\ (0) & =-3(0)+4 \\\\ 0 & =4\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let, for example,$$\n\\displaystyle a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$ from $L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0+1 \\\\ 4+1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHowever, $$\n\\displaystyle [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\notin L\n$$ since substituting the coordinates into the equation, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x+4 \\\\ (5) & =-3(1)+4 \\\\ 5 & =1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186564495 :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & \\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y=0 \\\\ \\frac{x}{y}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡y=0\\,⟹\\,y=1.\n$$\n\n \nThen, substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{1}=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,1)$ is an stationary point of $f(x,y).$  \n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{x}{y}\\right)=\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=\\frac{1}{y}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{x}{y}\\right)=-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & -\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,1)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nComputing the determinant $H(0,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,1)=det({f}^{″}(0,1))={0}^{2}-1\\cdot 1-=-1<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(0,1)$ is a saddle point.\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,1).$" "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since $$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & \\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y=0 \\\\ \\frac{x}{y}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡y=0\\,⟹\\,y=1.\n$$\n\n \nThen, substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{1}=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,1)$ is an stationary point of $f(x,y).$  \n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{x}{y}\\right)=\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=\\frac{1}{y}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{x}{y}\\right)=-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & -\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,1)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nComputing the determinant $H(0,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,1)=det({f}^{″}(0,1))={0}^{2}-1\\cdot 1-=-1<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(0,1)$ is a saddle point.\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,1).$"] [:db/cas 17592186564499 :question/problem "Let\n$T=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nbe the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ Then $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto" "Let\n$$\n\\displaystyle T=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\nbe the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ Then $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto"] [:db/cas 17592186564506 :question/problem "The function \n$f(x,y)$\nhas the second derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $H(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})<0$\n\nII. $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})>0$\n\nIII. The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ is a local maximum of $f$" "The function \n$f(x,y)$\nhas the second derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $H(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})<0$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})>0\n$$\n\nIII. The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ is a local maximum of $f$"] [:db/cas 17592186564506 :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ we obtain   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})=det({f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}))=(-1)^{2}-{0}^{2}=1>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})={({f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}))}_{11}=-1<0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})<0,$ the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ is a local maximum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ we obtain   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})=det({f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}))=(-1)^{2}-{0}^{2}=1>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})={({f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}))}_{11}=-1<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})>0$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})<0,\n$$ the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ is a local maximum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186564519 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ 2x-3\\end{aligned}],\\,x\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of the zero-vector do not satisfy the equation $2x-y=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y & =3 \\\\ 2(0)-(0) & =3 \\\\ 0 & =3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ from $L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0+1 \\\\ -3-1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHowever, $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\notin L$ since substituting the coordinates into the equation, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y & =3 \\\\ 2(1)-(-4) & =3 \\\\ 6 & =3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ 2x-3\\end{aligned}],\\,x\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of the zero-vector do not satisfy the equation $2x-y=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y & =3 \\\\ 2(0)-(0) & =3 \\\\ 0 & =3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let, for example, $$\n\\displaystyle a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$ from $L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0+1 \\\\ -3-1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHowever, $$\n\\displaystyle [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\notin L\n$$ since substituting the coordinates into the equation, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y & =3 \\\\ 2(1)-(-4) & =3 \\\\ 6 & =3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""] [:db/cas 17592186564529 :question/problem "The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]?$" "The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n$$\n\\displaystyle {a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186564531 :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}9 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{R}x & =\\overbrace{\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 8\\end{aligned}]}^{{Q}^{T}}\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}^{b} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}9 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]x & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}9{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2}=-9 \\\\ {x}_{2}=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=9.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}_{1}+8(9) & =-9 \\\\ 9{x}_{1} & =-81 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $Ax=b$ is $[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}].$" "The least-squares solution $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}9 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{R}x & =\\overbrace{\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 8\\end{aligned}]}^{{Q}^{T}}\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}^{b} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}9 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]x & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}9{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2}=-9 \\\\ {x}_{2}=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=9.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}_{1}+8(9) & =-9 \\\\ 9{x}_{1} & =-81 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $Ax=b$ is $$\n\\displaystyle [\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186564532 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t)+{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the missing coefficients in the solution to the initial value problem, shown above.\n\n$x(t)=$ \n{{field-1}} \n$\\,\\cdot \\,[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,-$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\,\\cdot \\,[\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t)+{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the missing coefficients in the solution to the initial value problem, shown above.\n\n$x(t)=$ \n{{field-1}} \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\cdot \\,[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,-\n$$\n{{field-2}}\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\cdot \\,[\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186564617 :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$, where\n\n$$\nr=1,\\,\\theta ={315}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡({315}^{∘}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡({315}^{∘}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$" "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$, where\n\n$$\nr=1,\\,\\theta ={315}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡({315}^{∘}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡({315}^{∘}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $$\n\\displaystyle (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186564650 :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$, where\n\n$$\nr=1,\\,\\theta ={45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡({45}^{∘}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡({45}^{∘}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$" "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$, where\n\n$$\nr=1,\\,\\theta ={45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡({45}^{∘}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡({45}^{∘}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $$\n\\displaystyle (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186564664 :question/problem "![](images/42/42e881f2fea91ee9191c5b32dce49f485977ded98a9e900bfcaf51fc1e0f8ec1.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path shown above." "![](images/42/42e881f2fea91ee9191c5b32dce49f485977ded98a9e900bfcaf51fc1e0f8ec1.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the path shown above."] [:db/cas 17592186564664 :question/worked-solution "The path $C$ is made up of two parts, as shown below:\n\n![](images/49/494158fe124fe1c8e1f7f618695dc9846991bd074b199a45a5874ddb8ad16e86.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path shown above.\n\nNow, we make use of the following facts:\n\n- If $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ is a piecewise-smooth curve, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ is a quarter of a circle of radius $r=\\frac{34}{3}.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\,\\text{d}s=\\frac{1}{4}(2\\pi r)=\\frac{17 \\pi}{3} .\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{2}$ is the hypotenuse of a right triangle where both legs have a length of $\\frac{34}{3}.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\,\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{\\frac{34}{3}}^{2}+{\\frac{34}{3}}^{2}}=\\frac{34 \\sqrt{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=\\frac{17 \\pi}{3}+\\frac{34 \\sqrt{2}}{3}=\\frac{17}{3}(\\pi+2\\sqrt{2}).\n$$" "The path $C$ is made up of two parts, as shown below:\n\n![](images/49/494158fe124fe1c8e1f7f618695dc9846991bd074b199a45a5874ddb8ad16e86.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the path shown above.\n\nNow, we make use of the following facts:\n\n- If $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ is a piecewise-smooth curve, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ is a quarter of a circle of radius $r=\\frac{34}{3}.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\,\\text{d}s=\\frac{1}{4}(2\\pi r)=\\frac{17 \\pi}{3} .\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{2}$ is the hypotenuse of a right triangle where both legs have a length of $\\frac{34}{3}.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\,\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{\\frac{34}{3}}^{2}+{\\frac{34}{3}}^{2}}=\\frac{34 \\sqrt{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=\\frac{17 \\pi}{3}+\\frac{34 \\sqrt{2}}{3}=\\frac{17}{3}(\\pi+2\\sqrt{2}).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186564668 :question/problem "![](images/00/00c1e8199146ddfdc1d8243eccfc5d5f44f9e67474722365a5c39c4a2226dcab.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path shown above." "![](images/00/00c1e8199146ddfdc1d8243eccfc5d5f44f9e67474722365a5c39c4a2226dcab.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the path shown above."] [:db/cas 17592186564670 :question/problem "![](images/69/69ff60a180694c898c1ae208d6e2dd95ef82ec0f27b7e4c1cc0256a04ae696b9.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is a path that traverses a semicircle of radius $6$ once in the counterclockwise direction, as shown above." "![](images/69/69ff60a180694c898c1ae208d6e2dd95ef82ec0f27b7e4c1cc0256a04ae696b9.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is a path that traverses a semicircle of radius $6$ once in the counterclockwise direction, as shown above."] [:db/cas 17592186564672 :question/problem "![](images/2a/2ae1eeb848f8d01597ec509c095d8a0307805640690d9d65102909e2