eft(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right).\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)$  must be *positive* because the sine ratio is *always* positive in the $2$nd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186567350 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int}_{C}F\\cdot\\text{d}r$ given that $F(x,y)=(- 2 x)\\,i+(- 2 y + 2)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j$ for $t\\in[\\frac{\\pi}{2},2 \\pi].$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int}_{C}F\\cdot\\text{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y)=(- 2 x)\\,i+(- 2 y + 2)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j$ for $t\\in[\\frac{\\pi}{2},2 \\pi].$"] [:db/cas 17592186567354 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int}_{C}F\\cdot\\text{d}r$ given that $F(x,y)=(x^{2} y)\\,i+(x + 1)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(2 t)\\,i+(t + 1)\\,j$ for $t\\in[0,1].$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int}_{C}F\\cdot\\text{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y)=(x^{2} y)\\,i+(x + 1)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(2 t)\\,i+(t + 1)\\,j$ for $t\\in[0,1].$"] [:db/cas 17592186567356 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int}_{C}F\\cdot\\text{d}r$ given that $F(x,y)=(5 x)\\,i+(5 y - 5)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j$ for $t\\in[\\frac{\\pi}{2},2 \\pi].$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int}_{C}F\\cdot\\text{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y)=(5 x)\\,i+(5 y - 5)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j$ for $t\\in[\\frac{\\pi}{2},2 \\pi].$"] [:db/cas 17592186567365 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ given that $F(x,y)=(-5)\\left(\\frac{y+1}{x}\\,i-\\frac{2(x-1)}{y}\\,j\\right)$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)={t}^{2}\\,i+(t-1)\\,j$ for $t\\in [3,4].$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle F(x,y)=(-5)\\left(\\frac{y+1}{x}\\,i-\\frac{2(x-1)}{y}\\,j\\right)\n$$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)={t}^{2}\\,i+(t-1)\\,j$ for $t\\in [3,4].$"] [:db/cas 17592186567368 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ given that $F(x,y)=(-1)\\left((y\\operatorname{\\sin}x)\\,i+(x+y)\\,j\\right)$ and $C$ is the path  $r(t)=\\operatorname{arcsin}t\\,i+2j$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{1}{2}].$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y)=(-1)\\left((y\\operatorname{\\sin}x)\\,i+(x+y)\\,j\\right)$ and $C$ is the path  $r(t)=\\operatorname{arcsin}t\\,i+2j$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{1}{2}].$"] [:db/cas 17592186567610 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ given that $F(x,y,z)=\\left(- 2 y z\\right)\\,i+\\left(- 2 x z\\right)\\,j+\\left(- 2 x y\\right)\\,k$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=\\left(t\\right)\\,i+\\left(t^{2}\\right)\\,j+\\left(t^{3}\\right)\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,1]$." "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y,z)=\\left(- 2 y z\\right)\\,i+\\left(- 2 x z\\right)\\,j+\\left(- 2 x y\\right)\\,k$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=\\left(t\\right)\\,i+\\left(t^{2}\\right)\\,j+\\left(t^{3}\\right)\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,1]$."] [:db/cas 17592186567619 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ given that $F(x,y,z)=\\left(x + z\\right)\\,i+\\left(- y + z\\right)\\,j+\\left(z^{2}\\right)\\,k$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=\\left(- 3 t^{2}\\right)\\,i+\\left(t^{2}\\right)\\,j+\\left(- 2 t\\right)\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,2]$." "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y,z)=\\left(x + z\\right)\\,i+\\left(- y + z\\right)\\,j+\\left(z^{2}\\right)\\,k$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=\\left(- 3 t^{2}\\right)\\,i+\\left(t^{2}\\right)\\,j+\\left(- 2 t\\right)\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,2]$."] [:db/cas 17592186567622 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ given that $F(x,y,z)=\\left(- 2 x\\right)\\,i+\\left(- 2 z\\right)\\,j+\\left(- 2 y\\right)\\,k$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=\\left(2 e^{t}\\right)\\,i+\\left(3 e^{2 t}\\right)\\,j+\\left(e^{- 2 t}\\right)\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,1]$." "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y,z)=\\left(- 2 x\\right)\\,i+\\left(- 2 z\\right)\\,j+\\left(- 2 y\\right)\\,k$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=\\left(2 e^{t}\\right)\\,i+\\left(3 e^{2 t}\\right)\\,j+\\left(e^{- 2 t}\\right)\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,1]$."] [:db/cas 17592186567641 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ given that $F(x,y,z)=\\left(- \\frac{3 z}{x}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{3 z}{y}\\right)\\,j+\\left(-3\\right)\\,k$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=\\left(e^{t}\\right)\\,i+\\left(e^{t}\\right)\\,j+\\left(t\\right)\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,1]$." "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle F(x,y,z)=\\left(- \\frac{3 z}{x}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{3 z}{y}\\right)\\,j+\\left(-3\\right)\\,k\n$$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=\\left(e^{t}\\right)\\,i+\\left(e^{t}\\right)\\,j+\\left(t\\right)\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,1]$."] [:db/cas 17592186567642 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ given that $F(x,y,z)=\\left(y\\right)\\,i+\\left(x\\right)\\,j+\\left(z\\right)\\,k$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=\\left(- e^{- 3 t}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- 2 e^{3 t}\\right)\\,j+\\left(- 2 e^{3 t}\\right)\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,1]$." "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y,z)=\\left(y\\right)\\,i+\\left(x\\right)\\,j+\\left(z\\right)\\,k$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=\\left(- e^{- 3 t}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- 2 e^{3 t}\\right)\\,j+\\left(- 2 e^{3 t}\\right)\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,1]$."] [:db/cas 17592186569965 :question/problem "Given that \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -16 & 10\\end{bmatrix}$ \nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" "Given that \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -16 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ \nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$"] [:db/cas 17592186569998 :question/problem "Given that\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.$ \nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then $L=$" "Given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ \nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then $L=$"] [:db/cas 17592186570015 :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/d9/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${45}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$" "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/d9/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${45}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $$\n\\displaystyle (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).\n$$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186570027 :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)}=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}=-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$" "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)}=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}=-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186570034 :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x} & =11x\\cdot {5}^{x}+4\\cdot {5}^{x} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x}-11x\\cdot {5}^{x}-4\\cdot {5}^{x} & =0 \\\\ (3{x}^{2}-11x-4)\\cdot {5}^{x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}-11x-4=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{5}^{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $3{x}^{2}-11x-4=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(3x+1)(x-4)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-\\frac{1}{3},4.$\n\n- The equation ${5}^{x}=0$ has no solutions because ${5}^{x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-\\frac{1}{3},4,$ and their product is $\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot 4=-\\frac{4}{3}.$" "First, we factor the equation:\n\n  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x} & =11x\\cdot {5}^{x}+4\\cdot {5}^{x} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x}-11x\\cdot {5}^{x}-4\\cdot {5}^{x} & =0 \\\\ (3{x}^{2}-11x-4)\\cdot {5}^{x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}-11x-4=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{5}^{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $3{x}^{2}-11x-4=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(3x+1)(x-4)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-\\frac{1}{3},4.$\n\n- The equation ${5}^{x}=0$ has no solutions because ${5}^{x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-\\frac{1}{3},4,$ and their product is $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot 4=-\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186570747 :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Applying this formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-5{x}^{2}+8) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-5\\cdot 2x+0 \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-10x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We know that $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$ Applying this formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-5{x}^{2}+8) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-5\\cdot 2x+0 \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-10x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186570750 :question/problem "Find the derivative of $y=\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{3}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}.$" "Find the derivative of $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{3}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186570750 :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Applying this formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{3}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{3}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{3}+{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We know that $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$ Applying this formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{3}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{3}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{3}+{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186570758 :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Applying this formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}-11) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We know that $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$ Applying this formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}-11) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186571376 :question/problem "$\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 5 & 2\\end{vmatrix}=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 5 & 2\\end{vmatrix}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186571383 :question/problem "$\\frac{{m}^{3}}{2n}\\cdot \\frac{4{n}^{2}}{{m}^{2}}=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{m}^{3}}{2n}\\cdot \\frac{4{n}^{2}}{{m}^{2}}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186571391 :question/problem "Find the scale factor of the transformation represented by the rotation-scaling matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find the scale factor of the transformation represented by the rotation-scaling matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186571396 :question/problem "$\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 7 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix}=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 7 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186571404 :question/problem "Calculate the determinant of the matrix\n$T=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 & -25\\end{bmatrix}$" "Calculate the determinant of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle T=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 & -25\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186571408 :question/problem "Find the cofactor of ${x}_{32}$ in \n$X=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 6 & -2 & 8 \\\\ -6 & 3 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find the cofactor of ${x}_{32}$ in \n$$\n\\displaystyle X=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 6 & -2 & 8 \\\\ -6 & 3 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186571413 :question/problem "$\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 2 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 2 & -8\\end{vmatrix}=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}5 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 2 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 2 & -8\\end{vmatrix}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186571421 :question/problem "Find the minor of  ${e}_{15}$ in  $E=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 & -3 & 8 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 11 & 5 & 4 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -6 & 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find the minor of  ${e}_{15}$ in  $$\n\\displaystyle E=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 & -3 & 8 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 11 & 5 & 4 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -6 & 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186571435 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\frac{m}{{n}^{2}m+2{n}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{2m+4}{m}?$" "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{m}{{n}^{2}m+2{n}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{2m+4}{m}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186571440 :question/problem "$\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 2 & -3 & 9 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}5 & 2 & -3 & 9 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186571443 :question/problem "Calculate the determinant of the matrix\n$W=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Calculate the determinant of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle W=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186571448 :question/problem "Consider the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is upper triangular\n\nII. $A$ is lower triangular\n\nIII. ${A}^{T}$ is lower triangular" "Consider the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is upper triangular\n\nII. $A$ is lower triangular\n\nIII. ${A}^{T}$ is lower triangular"] [:db/cas 17592186571454 :question/problem "Calculate\n$\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 & 5\\end{vmatrix}.$" "Calculate\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 & 5\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186572115 :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y),$ calculate $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y}$ given that $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$" "For the function $f(x,y),$ calculate $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y}$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186572117 :question/problem "Find the determinant of the matrix \n$N=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{6} & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & \\sqrt{5} & 2 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find the determinant of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle N=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{6} & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & \\sqrt{5} & 2 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186572145 :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y),$ calculate $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y}$ given that $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{\\sqrt{2x+{y}^{3}}}.$" "For the function $f(x,y),$ calculate $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y}$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{\\sqrt{2x+{y}^{3}}}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186572154 :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{cos}^{2}⁡(8\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits. \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta$ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that the first petal lies between the first and fourth quadrants. So it's convenient to assume that $\\theta$ can be negative.\n\nWe now solve the above equation for $8\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n8\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{16},\\frac{\\pi }{16}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $\\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{16},\\frac{\\pi }{16}].$ Notice that this interval lies between the first and fourth quadrants, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n2{\\int }_{-\\pi /16}^{\\pi /16}{cos}^{2}⁡(8\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{cos}^{2}⁡(8\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits. \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta$ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that the first petal lies between the first and fourth quadrants. So it's convenient to assume that $\\theta$ can be negative.\n\nWe now solve the above equation for $8\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n8\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{16},\\frac{\\pi }{16}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $$\n\\displaystyle \\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{16},\\frac{\\pi }{16}].\n$$ Notice that this interval lies between the first and fourth quadrants, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n2{\\int }_{-\\pi /16}^{\\pi /16}{cos}^{2}⁡(8\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186572159 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}\\frac{3n+2}{6}.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}\\frac{3n+2}{6}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186572165 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\sum }_{a=1}^{3}a{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{a+1}=${{field-1}}" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{a=1}^{3}a{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{a+1}=\n$${{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186572188 :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{cos}^{2}⁡(10\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits. \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta$ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that the first petal lies between the first and fourth quadrants. So it's convenient to assume that $\\theta$ can be negative.\n\nWe now solve the above equation for $10\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n10\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{20},\\frac{\\pi }{20}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $\\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{20},\\frac{\\pi }{20}].$ Notice that this interval lies between the first and fourth quadrants, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n2{\\int }_{-\\pi /20}^{\\pi /20}{cos}^{2}⁡(10\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{cos}^{2}⁡(10\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits. \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta$ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that the first petal lies between the first and fourth quadrants. So it's convenient to assume that $\\theta$ can be negative.\n\nWe now solve the above equation for $10\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n10\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{20},\\frac{\\pi }{20}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $$\n\\displaystyle \\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{20},\\frac{\\pi }{20}].\n$$ Notice that this interval lies between the first and fourth quadrants, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n2{\\int }_{-\\pi /20}^{\\pi /20}{cos}^{2}⁡(10\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186572670 :question/problem "Given that $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{x}},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x}{{2}^{x}}\\cdot ($\n{{field-1}}\n$).$" "Given that $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{x}},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x}{{2}^{x}}\\cdot (\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$).$"] [:db/cas 17592186572695 :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\frac{4}{3}a+\\lambda b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $\\frac{4}{3}a.$ So, we first draw the vector $\\frac{4}{3}a$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/b8/b8db5015e22e55282e180b1d81f7a2e1ca544dfe0f468dc4dedb6283bac2da5c.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $\\frac{4}{3}a.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=\\frac{1}{4}|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=\\frac{1}{4}b.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}a+\\frac{1}{4}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\frac{4}{3}a+\\lambda b=\\frac{4}{3}a+\\frac{1}{4}b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $b,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$" "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\frac{4}{3}a+\\lambda b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $\\frac{4}{3}a.$ So, we first draw the vector $\\frac{4}{3}a$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/b8/b8db5015e22e55282e180b1d81f7a2e1ca544dfe0f468dc4dedb6283bac2da5c.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $\\frac{4}{3}a.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=\\frac{1}{4}|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=\\frac{1}{4}b.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}a+\\frac{1}{4}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $$\n\\displaystyle c=\\frac{4}{3}a+\\lambda b=\\frac{4}{3}a+\\frac{1}{4}b.\n$$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $b,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186573250 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to \\,4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)+\\pi )=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to \\,4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)+\\pi )=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186573257 :question/problem "For the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x),$ you are given the following data:\n\n$$\nf(-2)=1,\\,{f}^{′}(-2)=-3,\\,g(-2)=2,\\,{g}^{′}(-2)=6\n$$\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\\right)$ at $x=-2.$" "For the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x),$ you are given the following data:\n\n$$\nf(-2)=1,\\,{f}^{′}(-2)=-3,\\,g(-2)=2,\\,{g}^{′}(-2)=6\n$$\n\nCompute $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\\right)\n$$ at $x=-2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186573271 :question/problem "Given that $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0